#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Roads]"
msgstr "Road Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Roads]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects how many **road maintenance vehicles** are currently in use."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Electricity]"
msgstr "Electricity Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Electricity]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects the electricity **output levels** of power plants and the **charging rate** of batteries in battery stations."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Water & Sewage]"
msgstr "Water & Sewage Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Water & Sewage]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects the **water output** levels of water pumping stations as well as the **sewage treatment** levels of sewage buildings."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Health & Deathcare]"
msgstr "Healthcare & Deathcare Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Health & Deathcare]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency**.\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects citizens' **recovery from illnesses and injuries**. It also affects how many **ambulances** are currently in use.\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects how many **hearses** are currently in use as well as the **processing speed** of bodies in the Crematorium.\r\n\r\nEfficiency also affects the **city-wide effects** of the Disease Control Center and Health Research Institute."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Garbage Management]"
msgstr "Garbage Management Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Garbage Management]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects the **processing speed** of garbage as well as how many **garbage trucks** are currently in use.\r\n\r\nEfficiency also affects the **electricity output** level of the Incineration Plant and the **city-wide effects** of the Industrial Waste Processing Site."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Education & Research]"
msgstr "Education & Research Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Education & Research]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects the **graduation chance** of students.\r\n\r\nEfficiency also affects the **city-wide effects** of the Technical University, Medical University, Radio Telescope, Geological Research Center, and the Large Hadron Collider."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Fire & Rescue]"
msgstr "Fire & Rescue Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Fire & Rescue]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects how many **fire engines** are currently in use.\r\n\r\nEfficiency also affects the **city-wide effect** of the Early Disaster Warning System."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Police & Administration]"
msgstr "Police & Administration Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Police & Administration]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects how many **police cars** are currently in use.\r\n\r\nEfficiency also affects the **city-wide effects** of the Central Intelligence Bureau Field Office, City Hall, and the Central Bank."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Transportation]"
msgstr "Transportation Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Transportation]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects how many **public transportation vehicles** are currently in use.\r\n\r\nEfficiency also affects the **city-wide effect** of the ChirpX Space Center."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Parks & Recreation]"
msgstr "Parks & Recreation Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Parks & Recreation]"
msgstr "The service budget affects service building **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects how many **park maintenance vehicles** are currently in use.\r\n\r\nEfficiency also affects the **attractiveness value** of the buildings."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_TITLE[Communications]"
msgstr "Communications Budget"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICE_BUDGET_SLIDER_DESCRIPTION[Communications]"
msgstr "The service budget affects the service buildings' **efficiency.**\r\n\r\nEfficiency affects the range and capacity of networks and how many post vans and trucks are currently in use.\r\n\r\nEfficiency also affects the city-wide effects of the Satellite Uplink."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICES_RESOURCE_FEE_DESCRIPTION[Electricity]"
msgstr "Citizens and businesses pay a service fee for using electricity, which offsets some of its production cost.\r\n\r\nThe electricity service fee affects the **overall electricity consumption rate** of the city. It also affects the happiness of citizens and companies' ability to do business."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.SERVICES_RESOURCE_FEE_DESCRIPTION[Water]"
msgstr "Citizens and businesses pay a service fee for using water, which offsets some its production cost.\r\n\r\nThe water service fee affects the **overall water consumption rate** and **sewage generation** of the city. It also affects the happiness of citizens and companies' ability to do business."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.TAX_AREA_DESCRIPTION[Residential]"
msgstr "Residential taxes are collected from citizens' monthly wages.\r\n\r\nTax rates can be set individually based on each citizen education level.\r\n\r\nLow taxes encourage citizens to move into the city. Lower taxes also help residential buildings level up faster, since more money is left over after upkeep expenses are paid.\r\n\r\nHigher taxes bring more money into the city’s treasury, but can drive away the citizens not willing to pay as much. Higher taxes also slow down residential building level ups, as there is less money left for improvement after expenses are paid. "

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.TAX_AREA_DESCRIPTION[Commercial]"
msgstr "Commercial taxes are collected from companies' monthly profits.\r\n\r\nTax rates can be set individually based on the different types of goods sold.\r\n\r\nLow taxes increase companies' chances of becoming financially successful. Lower taxes also help commercial buildings level up faster, since more money is left over after upkeep expenses are paid.\r\n\r\nHigher taxes bring more money into the city’s treasury, but can also cause companies problems by cutting into their profit margin. Higher taxes also slow down commercial building level ups, as there is less money left for improvement after expenses are paid."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.TAX_AREA_DESCRIPTION[Industrial]"
msgstr "Industrial taxes are collected from companies' monthly profits.\r\n\r\nTax rates can be set individually based on the different types of goods manufactured and sold.\r\n\r\nLow taxes increase companies' chances of becoming financially successful. Lower taxes also help industrial buildings level up faster, since more money is left over after upkeep expenses are paid.\r\n\r\nHigher taxes bring more money into the city’s treasury, but can also cause companies problems by cutting into their profit margin. Higher taxes also slow down industrial building level ups, as there is less money left for improvement after expenses are paid."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.TAX_AREA_DESCRIPTION[Office]"
msgstr "Office taxes are collected from companies' monthly profits.\r\n\r\nTax rates can be set individually based on different types of immaterial goods manufactured and sold.\r\n\r\nLow taxes increase companies' chances of becoming financially successful. Lower taxes also help office buildings level up faster, since more money is left over after upkeep expenses are paid.\r\n\r\nHigher taxes bring more money into the city’s treasury, but can also cause companies problems by cutting into their profit margin. Higher taxes also slow down office building level ups, as there is less money left for improvement after expenses are paid."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.TAX_RANGE"
msgstr "{MIN} – {MAX} %"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM[Taxes]"
msgstr "Taxes"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM_DESCRIPTION[Taxes]"
msgstr "Taxes are collected from households and companies.\r\n\r\nResidential taxes are based on monthly wages. Company taxes are based on monthly profits.\r\n\r\nLow taxes encourage new citizens to move into the city, and increase companies' chances of becoming successful. Low taxes also help buildings level up faster, as more money is left over after the building upkeep expenses are paid. \r\n\r\nHigh taxes bring in more money for the city, but can drive away citizens, and they cut into companies' profits. High taxes also make building leveling up slower, since there is less cash left after paying for upkeep expenses."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM[Fees]"
msgstr "Service Fees"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM_DESCRIPTION[Fees]"
msgstr "Fees are paid by households and companies in exchange for using various services such as education, healthcare, and utilities.\r\n\r\nHouseholds and companies adjust their service usage based on how high the fees are. While lower fees increase well-being in the city, they may not be enough to cover the service’s operating cost entirely. This leaves the city to pay for the rest."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM[Exports]"
msgstr "Service Trade"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM_DESCRIPTION[Exports]"
msgstr "Surplus from city services can be traded with surrounding cities in exchange for additional income.\r\n\r\nFor example, visitors can use the city’s healthcare services, and vacancies in schools can get filled by students from other cities. Surplus fresh water and sewage treatment capacity as well as excess electricity are automatically sold to surrounding cities as long as the proper utilities connections have been built (pipes for water and sewage, power lines for electricity). "

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM[Government]"
msgstr "Government Subsidies"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM_DESCRIPTION[Government]"
msgstr "The government automatically grants the city subsidies depending on its economic situation. A small city in an overall weak financial situation with poor tax revenue and low service fees receives a fair amount of subsidies, while a larger city in a good financial situation with high revenue stream receives only negligible to no subsidies."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM[Subsidies]"
msgstr "Subsidies"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM_DESCRIPTION[Subsidies]"
msgstr "Households and companies will receive city subsidies when their tax rates are set in the negative. This will give them a financial boost and exempt them from paying tax. With subsidies, households have more money to level up their building after paying for expenses. Subsidized companies have higher profit margins, and thus they are also able to spend more money on leveling up their businesses.\r\n\r\nSubsidies can be set on the **Taxation** tab, and they are adjustable based on citizen education levels for households and material and immaterial goods manufactured and sold for companies."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM[Upkeeps]"
msgstr "Service Upkeeps"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM_DESCRIPTION[Upkeeps]"
msgstr "Service upkeep costs consist of the service building’s operating costs (building maintenance and resources used to provide its service such as fuel for a power plant) and the wages of its employees.\r\n\r\nService buildings only provide the amount of each service required by the city at any given time, up to their maximum capacity. Accordingly, they only employ the required number of employees at all times. \r\n\r\nTo save on operating costs, it is possible to lower the quality of a service on the **Services** tab. This decreases the number of employees hired, but will also lead to poorer performance. For example, waiting times for ambulances will be longer, and police and fire departments will have increased response times due to short-staffing."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM[Imports]"
msgstr "Service Trade"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM_DESCRIPTION[Imports]"
msgstr "Certain services can be imported from surrounding cities. These include education, electricity, water and sewage, police and fire services, road maintenance, healthcare services and garbage services.\r\n\r\nImported services cost the city more than having them provided locally, but importing may be useful when the city is low on space, for example. The overall cost of an imported service is determined by actual usage amounts."

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM[Loan Interest]"
msgstr "Loan Interest"

#: en
msgid "EconomyPanel.BUDGET_ITEM_DESCRIPTION[Loan Interest]"
msgstr "If the city takes a loan, it needs to pay monthly interest based on the loaned amount.\r\n\r\nWhenever a new loan is taken, it is combined into any existing loan, and the monthly interest is calculated based on the combined total. Loan interest is a monthly running cost and does not reduce the loaned amount. Loans can be taken and paid back on the **Loans** tab."

