msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ServicesCategory]"
msgstr "SERVICES"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsGroup]"
msgstr "ROADS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorial]"
msgstr "Road Basics"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Roads"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "A city exists around roads.\r\n\r\n**When you build a road, a cell grid appears on the land next to it.** This is the area where you can build on.\r\n \r\n**Most roads also come with a built-in utility network.** \r\n\r\nA network of electric cables and pipes for water and sewage, so all buildings connected to a road with a utility network automatically have a connection to the utilities. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialRoadTypes]"
msgstr "Road Types"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialRoadTypes]"
msgstr "Roads come in **different sizes and configurations,** ranging from small alleys and one-way streets all the way to large highways.\r\n\r\nThe larger the road, the higher the speed limit and traffic capacity."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialTwoLaneRoad]"
msgstr "Two-lane Road"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialTwoLaneRoad]"
msgstr "The Two-lane Road is a basic road with two lanes, one in each direction, with street lamps on one side.\r\n\r\nIt is easy to build with, but it can only support a low amount of traffic.\r\n\r\n**When selecting a road, keep an eye on the information panel.**\r\nIt will show you how much the road costs to build and maintain, and how much electricity the built-in utility network can handle."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialToolOptions]"
msgstr "Tool Options"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialToolOptions]"
msgstr "These can be used to help you build exactly how and what you want. \r\n\r\n**Different options are available according to what you are currently building.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialToolOptionModes]"
msgstr "Tool Modes"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialToolOptionModes]"
msgstr "**Different modes help you build roads in varying shapes.** \r\n\r\nSelect the mode according to the shape you want. You can always switch to a different mode at any time."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialReplace]"
msgstr "Replace"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialReplace]"
msgstr "This mode can be used to change any road you have already built to a different type.\r\n\r\nNo need for the bulldozer!\r\n\r\n**Turn Replace mode on and  select a new road type from the Roads menu.**\r\n\r\n**When you select a section of an existing road on the map, the new road type will be built in the old one's place.** \r\n\r\nKeep an eye on how replacing affects traffic lanes and existing buildings."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialElevationDown]"
msgstr "Elevation Down"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialElevationDown]"
msgstr "Changing road elevation **down** allows you to construct **tunnels.**\r\n\r\nYou can construct tunnels at different elevations, which allows them to cross each other. This is especially handy when creating public transport lines."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialElevationUp]"
msgstr "Elevation Up"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialElevationUp]"
msgstr "Changing road elevation **up** allows you to construct **overpasses** and **ramps.**\r\n\r\nYou can also use it to bridge gaps in the terrain created by water or other obstacles."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialElevationStep]"
msgstr "Elevation Step"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialParallel]"
msgstr "Parallel Mode"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialParallel]"
msgstr "**This mode makes two of the selected roads run perfectly aligned next to each other.**\r\n\r\nYou can increase or decrease the space between the roads once you turn the mode on."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialToolOptionSnapping]"
msgstr "Snapping"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialToolOptionSnapping]"
msgstr "These options can help fine-tune the shape and placement of your roads. \r\n\r\n**With snapping on, roads and other objects become magnetic to what snapping modes you have turned on.**\r\n\r\nSnapping options can be turned on and off individually."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialPlacement]"
msgstr "Building Roads"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialPlacement]"
msgstr "To get your city started and to make it grow, you will need to make it possible for both people and materials to arrive.\r\n\r\n**Look around your map and find the roads that lead in and out of the map area.**\r\n\r\n**Start building your city by connecting to and expanding from these roads.**\r\n\r\nBuild your road with <inputAction:Tool/Apply> \r\n\r\nWhen done, unselect the road tool with  <inputAction:Tool/Secondary Apply> or <inputAction:Navigation/Back> \r\n\r\nHaving good connections to the outside world is key to your success!\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialPlacementGamepad]"
msgstr "To get your city started and to make it grow, you will need to make it possible for both people and materials to arrive.\r\n\r\n**Look around your map and find the roads that lead in and out of the map area.**\r\n\r\n**Start building your city by connecting to and expanding from these roads.**\r\n\r\nBuild your roads with <inputAction:Tool/Apply>\r\n\r\nWhen done, unselect the road tool with <inputAction:Navigation/Back>\r\n\r\nHaving good connections to the outside world is key to your success!"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[RoadsTutorialPlacementTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Road"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorial]"
msgstr "Road Services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Road Services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadServicesTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Road maintenance and extra resources are available through the Road Services.**\r\n\r\nMaintenance is aimed at keeping the roads in good condition, while the resources include things like roadside decorations, sound barriers and traffic signs."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorialDepot]"
msgstr "Road Maintenance Depot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadServicesTutorialDepot]"
msgstr "**Road Maintenance Depot sends out vehicles to keep roads in good condition.**\r\n\r\nMaintenance vehicles clear roads of snow, wrecked vehicles, debris and do road repairs as needed.\r\n\r\nHaving your roads well-maintained reduces the likelihood of vehicle accidents and traffic jams."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorialTrafficLights]"
msgstr "Traffic Lights"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadServicesTutorialTrafficLights]"
msgstr "**You can set up traffic lights at an intersection to control the flow of traffic.**\r\n\r\nThis can be useful especially in small but busy intersections, where traffic on one side needs to wait for a long time."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorialStopSigns]"
msgstr "Stop Signs"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadServicesTutorialStopSigns]"
msgstr "**You can set up stop signs at an intersection to force traffic to stop before crossing.**\r\n\r\nThis can be useful for allowing traffic from one direction to have more chances of crossing the intersection."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorialNoTurning]"
msgstr "No Turning Left or Right"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadServicesTutorialNoTurning]"
msgstr "**You can control the direction of traffic flow with these signs.**\r\n\r\nPlace the sign on the road section leading to an intersection. Turning in the indicated direction is no longer allowed, and the lane markings change accordingly. \r\n\r\nBoth signs can be placed on the same road, prohibiting turning in the intersection altogether."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorialDecorations]"
msgstr "Roadside Decorations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadServicesTutorialDecorations]"
msgstr "**Decorations can be added onto existing roads to modify their look and feel.**\r\n\r\nYou can add them on one or both sides of the road, and different decorations can be added on top of each other.\r\n\r\n**Choose the type of decoration you want and select the road section and side of the road you'd like to place it to.**\r\n\r\n**Note:** Decorations will replace roadside parking spaces from the selected sections."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorialRemoveSign]"
msgstr "Placing and Removing Signs and Decorations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadServicesTutorialRemoveSign]"
msgstr "Choose the type of decoration you want and select the road section and side of the road you'd like to place it to.\r\n\r\n**Place** signs and decorations with <inputAction:Tool/Apply>\r\n\r\n**Remove** them with <inputAction:Tool/Secondary Apply>\r\n\r\nTo select more then one road section at a time hold down your selection and move over all sections you'd like to select.\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadServicesTutorialRemoveSignGamepad]"
msgstr "Choose the type of decoration you want and select the road section and side of the road you'd like to place it to.\r\n\r\n**Place** signs and decorations with <inputAction:Tool/Apply>\r\n\r\n **Remove** them with <inputAction:Tool/Secondary Apply>"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadLaneDirectionTutorial]"
msgstr "Road Lane Direction"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadLaneDirectionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Road Lane Direction"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadLaneDirectionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Traffic direction on one-way and asymmetric road lanes is assigned when you build the road.\r\n\r\n**Lane markings point from where you start building the road to where it ends.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadLaneDirectionTutorialChange]"
msgstr "Changing Lane Direction"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadLaneDirectionTutorialChange]"
msgstr "**You can change the direction of lanes by building the same type of road right on top of the old one, but in the opposite direction.**\r\n\r\nBuilding the same type of road on top of an old one doesn't cost you anything."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadLaneDirectionTutorialOneWayRoad]"
msgstr "One-Way Roads"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadLaneDirectionTutorialOneWayRoad]"
msgstr "One-way roads have **traffic flowing in a single direction on all lanes.**\r\n\r\nKeep an eye out for dead end roads when building them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadLaneDirectionTutorialAsymmetricRoad]"
msgstr "Asymmetric Road"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadLaneDirectionTutorialAsymmetricRoad]"
msgstr "**Asymmetric roads have more lanes going in one direction and fewer in the other.**\r\n\r\nWhen using them, be mindful not to cause traffic to take unnecessary U-turns because of lanes ending abruptly. Also, when connecting them to other road types, try to align them so that there are no unnecessary lane changes required."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityGroup]"
msgstr "ELECTRICITY"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorial]"
msgstr "Electricity"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Electricity Basics"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Almost everything in the city needs electricity to work.**\r\n\r\nA well-working electricity grid keeps citizens happy and services running."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialSourcesIntro]"
msgstr "Sources of Electricity"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialSourcesIntro]"
msgstr "**There is a variety of ways to generate electricity,** and each choice comes with its own pros and cons."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialCoalPowerPlantIntro]"
msgstr "Small Coal Power Plant"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialCoalPowerPlantIntro]"
msgstr "A Coal Power Plant is a great source of **high voltage electricity,** but it also generates **a lot of pollution.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialWindTurbineIntro]"
msgstr "Wind Turbine"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialWindTurbineIntro]"
msgstr "Wind power is a source of **green energy,** but the output is completely **reliant on weather.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialOutsideIntro]"
msgstr "Outside Source"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialOutsideIntro]"
msgstr "Electricity can also be **imported** from neighboring cities by using Power Lines. This requires no maintenance on your part, but buying electricity when the population grows can get  **expensive.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialSources]"
msgstr "Choose a Source of Electricity"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialSources]"
msgstr "**Select the source of electricity you want to start with and close this panel to move on.**\r\n\r\nYou can backtrack back to this panel if you change your mind.\r\n\r\nYou can also  build more and different types of electricity sources as you progress.\r\n\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialCoalPowerPlantPlacement]"
msgstr "Building a Coal Power Plant"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialCoalPowerPlantPlacement]"
msgstr "**Place the power plant downwind from housing and away from water sources to avoid polluting them.**\r\n\r\nThe visual overlays show wind direction and the locations of groundwater sources. \r\n\r\nAll power plants have an integrated transformer that converts the high voltage electricity produced by the plant into low voltage electricity that is usable by buildings. \r\n\r\n**When the power plant is placed next to a road, the transformer begins feeding the low voltage electric grid automatically through the road's integrated electric cabling.**\r\n\r\nThe plant also has a high voltage connection point at the back, which can be used later to create an efficient power grid by transferring electricity further with Power Lines.\r\n\r\nCoal Power Plants will require a steady supply of coal to remain operational. Good road access keeps the needed supplies coming in."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ElectricityTutorialCoalPowerPlantPlacementTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Coal Power Plant"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialWindTurbinePlacement]"
msgstr "Building a Wind Turbine"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialWindTurbinePlacement]"
msgstr "**Place Wind Turbines somewhere they have enough room and are away from buildings.**\r\n\r\nWind Turbines generate noise pollution that can disturb residents.\r\n\r\n**You can place more than one turbine close to each other to generate more electricity.**\r\n\r\nThe visual overlay displays current wind direction and strength with the arrows' orientation and length.\r\n\r\nOnce built, turbines will turn to face the wind automatically."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ElectricityTutorialWindTurbinePlacementTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Wind Turbine"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialWindElectricCable]"
msgstr "Electric Cable"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialWindElectricCable]"
msgstr "These low voltage cables can be used to **connect the turbines to each other, and to roads.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialWindElevation]"
msgstr "Elevation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialWindElevation]"
msgstr "You can **lower the elevation to run Electric Cables underground.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialWindTurbineConnection]"
msgstr "Connecting Turbines"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialWindTurbineConnection]"
msgstr "**Wind turbines produce low-voltage electricity, and can be connected directly to roads with Electric Cables.**\r\n\r\nCables can be run above ground or underground, and turbines can be daisy-chained to each other before connecting the cable to a road."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ElectricityTutorialWindTurbineConnectionTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect the wind turbine to a road with Electric Cable"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialOutsideTransformer]"
msgstr "Connecting to an Outside Source"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialOutsideTransformer]"
msgstr "**To get electricity from an outside source, you need to build a Transformer Station.**\r\n\r\nThey convert the high voltage electricity coming in from the Power Lines into low voltage electricity and then feed it into the electric cables running underneath roads to be used by buildings."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialOutsideTransformerPlacing]"
msgstr "Building a Transformer Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialOutsideTransformerPlacing]"
msgstr "**Place the Transformer Station next to a road in a central location to get equal distribution to buildings.**\r\n\r\nWell placed Transformer Stations can prevent bottlenecks from forming and keep the flow of electricity efficient."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ElectricityTutorialOutsideTransformerPlacingTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Transformer Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialOutsidePowerLine]"
msgstr "High-voltage Power Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialOutsidePowerLine]"
msgstr "High-voltage Power Lines have a high capacity, and they are **used to connect Transformer Stations and Power Plants.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialOutsideElevation]"
msgstr "Power Line Elevation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialOutsideElevation]"
msgstr "To avoid going over roads and buildings, **adjust the elevation down to build underground cables.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityTutorialOutsideConnecting]"
msgstr "Connecting Outside Power"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityTutorialOutsideConnecting]"
msgstr "**Move around your starting map tile and find the incoming Power Line somewhere on the edge.**\r\n\r\nRun a Power Line from it to the Transformer Station you built earlier.\r\n\r\nOnce the connection is established, electricity starts flowing in from a neighboring city."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ElectricityTutorialOutsideConnectingTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect Outside Power"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityFlowTutorial]"
msgstr "Electricity Flow"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityFlowTutorialSelectInfoView]"
msgstr "Electricity Info View"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityFlowTutorialSelectInfoView]"
msgstr "**Electricity info view shows an overlay of electricity flow throughout the city, to help you spot weaknesses and solve problems.**\r\n\r\nElectricity panel also shows detailed information about the performance of the electricity grid, conditions that affect it and where your electricity plants are located."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityFlowTutorialElectricityFlowing]"
msgstr "Electricity Flowing"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityFlowTutorialElectricityFlowing]"
msgstr "Electricity flow is animated to show how much electricity is flowing and where it's going.\r\n\r\n**Low amounts of electricity are shown as short bars. When the electricity flow increases, the bars grow longer.**\r\n\r\n**Note:**Individual buildings receive only the amount they need and most homes use only a small amount of electricity."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityFlowTutorialCableCapacity]"
msgstr "Cable Capacity"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityFlowTutorialCableCapacity]"
msgstr "**There is a limit to how much electricity Electric Cables and Power Lines are able to transfer.**\r\n\r\nWhen there is more electricity trying to flow through the cables and lines than they can handle, the electric grid will suffer and result in buildings not getting enough power.\r\n\r\n**Note:** While Electricity info view is active, you can hover over power lines and cables to see how much electricity is being transferred and how much the capacity is."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityFlowTutorialElectricityBottleneck]"
msgstr "Electricity Bottleneck"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityFlowTutorialElectricityBottleneck]"
msgstr "**When an Electric Cable or Line has exceeded maximum capacity, a bottleneck will form.**\r\n\r\nBottlenecks will start to block electricity flow and cause buildings down the line to not receive electricity.\r\n\r\nTo clear the bottleneck and resume electricity service, you will need to bypass the bottleneck by redirecting electricity flow."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityFlowTutorialDirectingFlow]"
msgstr "Directing Electricity Flow"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityFlowTutorialDirectingFlow]"
msgstr "**Electricity flow can be directed and diverted with Power Lines and Transformer Stations.**\r\n\r\nTransformer Stations can be placed in areas that need electricity, and connected to the Power Plant with Power Lines.\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityBottleneckTutorial]"
msgstr "Electricity Bottleneck"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Bottleneck has formed"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Somewhere there are Electric Cables or Lines that have exceeded their capacity and a Bottleneck has formed.**\r\n\r\nBuildings down the line from the bottleneck have been cut off and are not receiving the electricity they need.\r\n\r\n**Find the electric cable or line that is exceeding capacity.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialSolving]"
msgstr "Solving a Bottleneck"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialSolving]"
msgstr "You will need to bypass the bottleneck area **by redirecting electricity flow with a Transformer Station and some Power Lines.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialTransformerStation]"
msgstr "Build a Transformer Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialTransformerStation]"
msgstr "To bypass the affected area you will need to build a Transformer Station.\r\n\r\n**Place the Transformer Station next to a road past the bottleneck, in a central location to the buildings that are not receiving electricity.**\r\n\r\nIf there is a large area that is not receiving electricity, you can place several Transformer Stations throughout the area."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialTransformerStationTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Transformer Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialConnectPowerStation]"
msgstr "Connecting to a Power Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialConnectPowerStation]"
msgstr "**Connect the Transformer Station to a Power Plant with a Power Line.**\r\n\r\nWhen connected, the transformer starts feeding the grid beyond the bottleneck directly from the Power Station, and the bottleneck will be cleared.\r\n\r\n**Note:** If there are buildings and roads in the way, it is easier to build the Power Line underground."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ElectricityBottleneckTutorialsConnectPowerStationTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect Transformer Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[Water&SewageGroup]"
msgstr "WATER & SEWAGE"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorial]"
msgstr "Water"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Water"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WaterTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Every city needs a good water network.**\r\n\r\nHouseholds, companies and industries all need water to operate and survive.\r\n\r\nWhen the water system is working well, citizens stay healthier, and companies and services are able to operate without problems."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorialGroundwaterStation]"
msgstr "Groundwater Pumping Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WaterTutorialGroundwaterStation]"
msgstr "**Groundwater Pumping Station can be used if your current city limits contain underground water deposits.**\r\n\r\nGroundwater Pumping Stations are placed on the surface, above the water deposit  to provide clean water for the city."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorialPumpingStation]"
msgstr "Water Pumping Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WaterTutorialPumpingStation]"
msgstr "**Water Pumping Station can be used if your current city limits has access to open water.**\r\n\r\nWater Pumping Stations are placed on the shoreline of any open water to provide clean water for the city."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorialWaterSources]"
msgstr "Choose a Pumping Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WaterTutorialWaterSources]"
msgstr "**Select the type of pumping station you want to start with and close this panel to move on.**\r\n\r\nYou can come back to this panel if you change your mind. \r\n\r\nYou can also build more and different types of pumps later on."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorialPlaceGroundwater]"
msgstr "Build a Groundwater Pumping Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WaterTutorialPlaceGroundwater]"
msgstr "**Look around the map and find an area with a groundwater deposit.**\r\n\r\nGroundwater deposits are shown on the ground while the Water & Sewage info view is on.\r\n\r\n**Place the Groundwater Pumping Station over the deposit area.**\r\n\r\n**The darkest area is where there is the most water and where you'll be able to extract as much water as possible.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[WaterTutorialPlaceGroundwaterTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Groundwater Pumping Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorialConnectGroundwater]"
msgstr "Connecting  Groundwater Pumping Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WaterTutorialConnectGroundwater]"
msgstr "**Pumping Stations need a road connection.**\r\n\r\n**If your station is already built next to a road,** you're all set and the station is connected to the roads utility network.\r\n\r\n**If your station isn't connected to a road,** you'll need to build one.\r\n\r\nLook for the Road Required notification and zoom in to see which side of the building it's on, and build your road along that side. \r\n\r\nYou can use any road with a built-in pipe network. When the station is connected to the rest of the road system, water will be connected as well and start flowing."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "Building a Water Pumping Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WaterTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "Look around the map and locate a good water source.\r\n\r\n**Place the Water Pump on the shoreline.**\r\n\r\nMake sure the location is far enough away and upstream from sources of pollution, to keep the water pumped to the city clean and clear of contamination.\r\n\r\n**Arrows in the water show the direction and strength of the water flow.**\r\n\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[WaterTutorialPlaceTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Pumping Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTutorialConnectWater]"
msgstr "Connecting  Water Pumping Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WaterTutorialConnectWater]"
msgstr "**Pumping Stations need a road connection.**\r\n\r\n**If your station is already built next to a road,** you're all set and the station is connected to the roads utility network.\r\n\r\n**If your station isn't connected to a road,** you'll need to build one.\r\n\r\nLook for the Road Required notification and zoom in to see which side of the building it's on, and build your road along that side. \r\n\r\nYou can use any road with a built-in pipe network. When the station is connected to the rest of the road system, water will be connected as well and start flowing."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SewageTutorial]"
msgstr "Sewage"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SewageTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Sewage"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SewageTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**A good sewage network is essential to a city.**\r\n\r\nIf sewage is not properly managed, it can have negative effects on the city and its citizens.\r\n\r\n**Sewage pipes are included in the road utility network.** Separate pipes can be built to connect sewage outlets that are not connected to a road."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SewageTutorialOutlet]"
msgstr "Sewage Outlet"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SewageTutorialOutlet]"
msgstr "As clean water is used, it becomes sewage. \r\n\r\n**Sewage Outlets expel the wastewater from the water network.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SewageTutorialPlaceOutlet]"
msgstr "Building a Sewage Outlet"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SewageTutorialPlaceOutlet]"
msgstr "Find an area with open, flowing water.\r\n\r\n**Place the sewage outlet on the shoreline.**\r\n\r\nTo avoid polluting clean water sources,  place the outlet far from groundwater deposits and downstream from any Water Pumping Stations.\r\n\r\n**Note:** Sewage Outlets don't require electricity or a road connection to operate, but it will need to be connected to the sewage network with a pipe.\r\n\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[SewageTutorialPlaceOutletTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Sewage Outlet"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SewageTutorialPipe]"
msgstr "Sewage Pipe"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SewageTutorialPipe]"
msgstr "**A single Sewage Pipe** to connect Sewage Outlets to the roads utility network."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SewageTutorialConnectPipe]"
msgstr "Connecting the Sewage Outlet"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SewageTutorialConnectPipe]"
msgstr "**Find the nearest road that has the built in utility network.**\r\n\r\nSewage piping will be highlighted while the Sewage Pipe is selected and Water & Sewage info view is active.\r\n\r\n**Connect the Sewage Outlet to the road utility network with the Sewage Pipe.**\r\n\r\n**Note:** Highways don't have built in utility networks, and you can't connect to an individual building's pipe connection."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[SewageTutorialConnectPipeTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect the Sewage Outlet to the existing network."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[Health&DeathcareGroup]"
msgstr "HEALTHCARE & DEATHCARE"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[HealthcareTutorial]"
msgstr "Healthcare"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[HealthcareTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Healthcare"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[HealthcareTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Good healthcare system helps prevent diseases and untimely deaths by treating the sick and injured.**\r\n\r\nHealthcare services also keep citizens healthier and happier by giving advice and information. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[HealthcareTutorialIntroMedicalBuildings]"
msgstr "Clinics and Hospitals"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[HealthcareTutorialIntroMedicalBuildings]"
msgstr "**Sick citizens visit clinics and hospitals for treatment on their own or, if they are in serious condition, an ambulance will be sent out to pick them up.**\r\n\r\nMedical Clinics and Hospitals have a set of ambulances at their disposal, and a limited amount of room for patients. \r\n\r\nMedical buildings can be upgraded to increase the capacity of both."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[HealthcareTutorialMedicalClinic]"
msgstr "Building Healthcare services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[HealthcareTutorialMedicalClinic]"
msgstr "**Build medical facilities in central locations, close to the citizens, and with easy road access.**\r\n\r\nIf patients need to travel far to get treatment, it takes them longer to get better and the likelihood of deaths increases.\r\n\r\nEach Clinic and Hospital can only treat and care for a limited number of people. Opening the building info panel will guide you on how well a facility is working.\r\n\r\n**As your population increases, you will need to build more facilities.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[HealthcareTutorialMedicalClinicTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Medical Clinic"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DeathcareTutorial]"
msgstr "Deathcare"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DeathcareTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Deathcare"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DeathcareTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Each citizen will eventually pass away. **Deathcare services exist to take care of the deceased.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DeathcareTutorialCemetery]"
msgstr "Cemetery"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DeathcareTutorialCemetery]"
msgstr "**Cemeteries are large, park-like locations where deceased citizens are buried.**\r\n\r\nHearses pick up the deceased and transport them to the cemetery for burial."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DeathcareTutorialPlaceCemetery]"
msgstr "Building a Cemetery"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DeathcareTutorialPlaceCemetery]"
msgstr "Citizens like to visit Cemeteries. Cemeteries also increase the well-being of nearby citizens.\r\n \r\n**Find a nice place, close to housing, with good traffic connections to build your cemetery in.**\r\n\r\nEach Cemetery has a limited number of graves, so as your city grows, you will most likely need to build more than one."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[DeathcareTutorialPlaceCemeteryTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Cemetery"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GarbageManagementGroup]"
msgstr "GARBAGE MANAGEMENT"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GarbageTutorial]"
msgstr "GARBAGE MANAGEMENT"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GarbageTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Garbage Management"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[GarbageTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Good Garbage management system keeps citizens healthier and happier.\r\n\r\n**Garbage management facilities send out trucks that collect waste from around the city and bring it back for processing.**\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GarbageTutorialIntroLandfill]"
msgstr "Landfill"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[GarbageTutorialIntroLandfill]"
msgstr "Landfill is the basic Garbage Management facility. \r\n\r\n**Collected garbage is stored in the dumping area, also known as a lot.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GarbageTutorialLandfill]"
msgstr "Building a Landfill"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[GarbageTutorialLandfill]"
msgstr "Garbage Management buildings generate pollution in their vicinity, so take care when choosing a location.\r\n\r\n**Place the main building in a location that has plenty of room for the lot starting from the back of the building.**\r\n\r\nThe circle around the building marks how far you can extend the lot from the building.\r\n\r\n**Place the building and define the area for the lot.**\r\n\r\n**Note:** When the lot gets full you will need to start the emptying process from the building information panel. Information panels open by selecting the building.\r\n\r\nHaving a good road network across the city is important to maintain a good Garbage Management process."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[GarbageTutorialLandfillTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Landfill"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GarbageTutorialLandfillAdjustment]"
msgstr "Adjusting the lot size"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[GarbageTutorialLandfillAdjustment]"
msgstr "You can increase and decrease the capacity of a lot, or reshape it to manage area pollution.\r\n\r\n**To adjust the lot, select the Lot Tool. Grab a node on the lot and drag it to where you want it.**\r\n\r\nYou can also select the line between nodes to create a new node.\r\n\r\nYou can reshape the lot whenever you want."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[GarbageTutorialLandfillAdjustmentTrigger]"
msgstr "Adjust the Landfill lot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EducationGroup]"
msgstr "EDUCATION & RESEARCH"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EducationTutorial]"
msgstr "Education"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EducationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Education"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EducationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Education city services provide citizens with schools, colleges and universities.**\r\n\r\nCitizen education affects their job position and the income level of their household.\r\n\r\nCitizens have different preferences for their level of education, affected by their well-being and job opportunities in the city."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EducationTutorialIntroEducationLevels]"
msgstr "Education Levels"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EducationTutorialIntroEducationLevels]"
msgstr "**Citizens' education is separated into five levels, ranging from Uneducated to Highly Educated.**\r\n\r\nThe types of jobs available in the city and the number of open positions affect which educational services are needed."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EducationTutorialIntroEducationPath]"
msgstr "Education Path"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EducationTutorialIntroEducationPath]"
msgstr "**Education Path is citizens schooling from the lowest level to the highest level.**\r\n\r\nTo fill jobs that require a low level of education, the city only needs to provide lower level schooling facilities. \r\n\r\nTo provide employees for jobs with a higher education requirement, citizens need the entire education path from lower level schooling to higher education."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ResearchTutorial]"
msgstr "Research"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ResearchTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Research Facilities"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ResearchTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Research facilities advance and benefit the city in many ways.**\r\n\r\nThey help citizens graduate from higher education and aid in utilising natural resources."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ResearchTutorialScientist]"
msgstr "Scientists"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ResearchTutorialScientist]"
msgstr "**Research facilities need highly educated people.** \r\n\r\nFor Research Facilities to function well, it's beneficial for the city to invest in colleges and universities."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[Fire&RescueGroup]"
msgstr "FIRE & RESCUE"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FireAndRescueTutorial]"
msgstr "Fire & Rescue"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FireAndRescueTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Fire Department"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[FireAndRescueTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The Fire and Rescue city service **responds to fires and accidents, prevents fires from spreading, and safeguards citizens against disasters.**\r\n\r\nThey also search for survivors in collapsed buildings after a disaster."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FireAndRescueTutorialFireFighting]"
msgstr "Fire Fighting"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[FireAndRescueTutorialFireFighting]"
msgstr "Fires damage buildings and can quickly spread to other buildings nearby.\r\n\r\nIf the response time of the Fire and Rescue service is too long, buildings are at a greater risk of burning down.\r\n\r\n**To ensure the quickest response time, make sure roads stay clear and that there are enough Fire Engines available.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FireAndRescueTutorialFireTrucks]"
msgstr "Fire Engines"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[FireAndRescueTutorialFireTrucks]"
msgstr "Fire Houses and Fire Stations house a number of Fire Engines.\r\n\r\n**Fire Engines are able to extinguish multiple fires in succession before returning.**\r\n\r\nFire Engines can also extinguish Forest Fires that are close enough to the road."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FireAndRescueTutorialFireDamage]"
msgstr "Fire Damage"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FireAndRescueTutorialFireSafety]"
msgstr "Fire Safety"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[FireAndRescueTutorialFireSafety]"
msgstr "**The likelihood of fires in the city depends on the fire hazard levels.**\r\n\r\nYou can increase the safety of your city and its citizens by making sure all areas have good coverage. This means having enough Fire Houses and Stations in close proximity, and upgrading them as needed.\r\n\r\nThere are also Policies that you can activate to increase building safety."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DisasterControlTutorial]"
msgstr "Disaster Control"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DisasterControlTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Disaster Control"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DisasterControlTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Disaster Control service buildings **help both in detecting oncoming natural disasters and safely housing citizens** that have been affected by a disaster. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[Police&AdministrationGroup]"
msgstr "POLICE & ADMINISTRATION"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PoliceTutorial]"
msgstr "Police"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PoliceTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Police"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PoliceTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Crime is an unfortunate downside to building a community. \r\n\r\n**The purpose of law enforcement is to both prevent crime as well as to catch criminals.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PoliceTutorialResponse]"
msgstr "Patrol Cars"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PoliceTutorialResponse]"
msgstr "**Police Stations and Headquarters send out police cars to patrol the city and respond to criminal activity.**\r\n\r\nApprehended criminals are brought in and kept in holding cells to wait for further processing, such as a trial."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PoliceTutorialCrime]"
msgstr "Crime"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PoliceTutorialCrime]"
msgstr "**Low overall well-being and unemployment increase the likelihood of citizens becoming criminals.**\r\n\r\nSo in addition to investing in the police force, also make sure your citizens are doing well in order to prevent crime."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AdministrationTutorial]"
msgstr "Administration"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AdministrationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Administration Buildings"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[AdministrationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Administrative buildings **have a positive effect on citizen well-being, policies, trade, taxation, and the economy as a whole.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TransportationGroup]"
msgstr "TRANSPORTATION"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PublicTransportationTutorial]"
msgstr "Public Transportation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PublicTransportationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Public Transportation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PublicTransportationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Different means of public transportation offer convenient ways for citizens and tourists to move around and for goods to be delivered.\r\n\r\n**Well organised public transportation decreases traffic congestion and lowers the overall cost of transportation.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PublicTransportationTutorialPassenger]"
msgstr "Passenger Transportation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PublicTransportationTutorialPassenger]"
msgstr "**Using Busses,Trains, Ships and Airplanes for passengers lowers the cost of traveling for citizens and tourists.**\r\n\r\nPublic transportation makes it possible for more people to easily visit your city and bring in tourism revenue.\r\n\r\nCitizens using public transportation have more money to use on goods and services and, with good public transportation coverage, people can quickly move to where they need to go."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PublicTransportationTutorialCargo]"
msgstr "Cargo Transportation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PublicTransportationTutorialCargo]"
msgstr "**Using Trains, Ships and Airplanes for transporting cargo lowers the cost of shipping goods and materials.**\r\n\r\nThis results in more businesses that are profitable and able to provide jobs and pay taxes. \r\n\r\nWhen the production of goods and materials is going well in the city, any surplus can be exported for further profit."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadTransportGroup]"
msgstr "Road"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorial]"
msgstr "Bus"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Buses"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Buses are the easiest way to start a public transportation network in your city.**\r\n\r\nThey offer citizens and tourists a convenient way to get around and decrease traffic congestion."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialDepot]"
msgstr "Bus Depot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialDepot]"
msgstr "**To start a bus service you will first need a Bus Depot.**\r\n\r\nThe depot houses a fleet of buses and has facilities to handle their maintenance."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialPlaceDepot]"
msgstr "Build a Bus Depot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialPlaceDepot]"
msgstr "**A good place to build your Depot is somewhere away from high traffic, and with room to expand later.**\r\n\r\nDepots don't need to be in the immediate vicinity of the area where your buses operate.\r\n\r\nBus lines do not need to pass through depots, and a single depot can supply buses for multiple lines. \r\n\r\n**Make sure buses going out from the depot have road access to your planned route, and that it's still close enough for efficient operation.**\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[BusTutorialPlaceDepotTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Bus Depot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialStation]"
msgstr "Bus Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialStation]"
msgstr "**Bus Stations work as large bus stops, and a single station can accommodate several bus lines.**\r\n \r\nStations can also be upgraded and expanded to include Taxi services. \r\n\r\nBus Stations can handle a high number of passengers, but are not required to start a bus line."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialStop]"
msgstr "Bus Stops"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialStop]"
msgstr "To create a Bus Line you will need Bus Stops, which buses use to pick up and drop off passengers.\r\n\r\n**Bus Stops can be placed on either side of the road,** wherever you want  buses to make a stop along the line.\r\n\r\n**Bus Stops create a short stopping lane on the road** where they are placed."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialShelter]"
msgstr "Bus Shelter"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialShelter]"
msgstr "** Bus Shelter is an alternative to the Bus Stop,**\r\n\r\nShelters are more expensive to build, but provide passengers with a nicer place to wait for the bus, especially in bad weather."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialStyle]"
msgstr "Bus Stop Style"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialStyle]"
msgstr "**Changing the Theme will also change the style of bus stops and shelters.**\r\n\r\nThe change is not applied to previously built Bus Stops and Shelters."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialStopPlacement]"
msgstr "Bus Stop Placement"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialStopPlacement]"
msgstr "**To make a bus line, you need at least 2 stops.** Place these in locations that are convenient for citizens.\r\n\r\nStops can be either Bus Stops, Bus Shelters or Bus Stations.\r\n\r\n**Keep an eye on which side of the road you place your stops on, as it affects the route buses take.**\r\n\r\nStops can always be moved later in case you misplace them or wish to change where the line runs."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[BusTutorialStopTrigger]"
msgstr "Build at least 2 Bus Stops"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialRoute]"
msgstr "Bus Line Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialRoute]"
msgstr "**Bus Line Tool is used to connect Bus Stops, Shelters and Stations in a loop, creating a bus line between the selected stops.**\r\n\r\nThere is no limit to how many lines are able to use a particular stop, so several different lines can share stops."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialRoutePlacement]"
msgstr "Create a Bus Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialRoutePlacement]"
msgstr "**Select each stop, shelter and station, in the order you would like the Bus Line to operate.**\r\n\r\n**Complete the loop by selecting the stop that you started the line with.**\r\n\r\nThe line will automatically follow the correct traffic lanes. If you have stops on opposite sides of the road, the line will turn back around by using the closest available roads. \r\n\r\nWhen you have completed the line, **buses will automatically be sent out from the Bus Depot to begin operating the line.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[BusTutorialRouteTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect your Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialAddingStops]"
msgstr "Adding New Bus Stops"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialAddingStops]"
msgstr "You can add more stops to any line you've already created.\r\n\r\n**Place one or more stops or stations to where you wish the route to go.**\r\n\r\n**Then, select the Bus Line tool, grab your existing line at the nearest point to your new stop, and drag the line to the stop.**\r\n \r\nThe line will snap to the stop and the stop is now a part of the line.\r\n\r\nRepeat as needed to include more than one new stop."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialRelocateStop]"
msgstr "Relocating stops"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialRelocateStop]"
msgstr "To relocate a stop, **close the Transportation menu.**\r\n\r\n**Select the stop you want to move.** This will open the Line Information Panel.\r\n\r\nIn the Bus Stop's Information Panel, **select the relocate icon and move the stop to a new location.**\r\n\r\nAll Lines will stay with the stop and adjust according to the new location.\r\n\r\n**Note:**You can also select stops in the Line Information Panel."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialDeletingStops]"
msgstr "Removing Bus Stops"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialDeletingStops]"
msgstr "**You can remove bus stops with the bulldozer.**\r\n\r\nBulldozing a stop **doesn't affect the lines** that were using the stop.\r\n\r\n**Note:** If you have trouble locating the stops on the map, you can use the Line Information Panel to select them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusTutorialRerouting]"
msgstr "Rerouting Bus Lines"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusTutorialRerouting]"
msgstr "**If you would like your line to follow a different path from the one that is automatically created, you can add waypoints to the line.**\r\n\r\nWaypoints won't pick up passengers and don't need bus stops, they are there to adjust the route the buses take.\r\n\r\n**To add waypoints, select the Bus Line Tool. Then, grab the line at a convenient point,  and drag and drop it to where you want it.**\r\n\r\nA new waypoint is created. \r\n\r\nYou can continue to add and move waypoints by dragging and dropping them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusOutsideTutorial]"
msgstr "Bus Line Outside Connection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Bus Lines to other cities"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "To alleviate the traffic jams caused by people coming in to the city, **you can make Bus Line connections to the outside world.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BusOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "Connecting Bus Line to an Outside Connection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BusOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "**Move around and locate the outside connection marker at the edge of the map area.**\r\n\r\nYou don't need to own the map tiles all the way to the marker, you just need to have one tile with a part of the road that leads to the marker.\r\n\r\nYou will need at least one bus stop in your city. Make sure your bus stop is on a road that has access to the marker.\r\n\r\n**Select the Bus Line Tool and create a line by selecting one or more bus stops. Include the Outside Connection marker as a stop in your bus line.**\r\n\r\nYou can also add the Outside Connection marker to an existing line by dragging and dropping the line to the connection point."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[BusOutsideTutorialConnectTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect Bus Line to the Outside"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxiTutorial]"
msgstr "Taxi"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxiTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Taxi Service"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TaxiTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Taxis are a good transportation option for places that have no other public transportation nearby.\r\n\r\n**To get a taxi service going, all you need is a taxi depot.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxiTutorialDepot]"
msgstr "Build a Taxi Depot "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TaxiTutorialDepot]"
msgstr "**Taxi Depot is the center of operations for taxis.**\r\nIt houses a dispatch office, a fleet of taxis and a repair crew. \r\n\r\n**For good service, place the depot in a central location with room to expand.**\r\n\r\nAfter building a Taxi Depot, dispatchers start sending out taxis to customers. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TaxiTutorialDepotTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Taxi Depot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxiTutorialStandIntro]"
msgstr "Taxi Stands"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TaxiTutorialStandIntro]"
msgstr "For faster and more efficient taxi service, **Taxi Stands can be placed by the roadside around the city.** \r\n\r\nStands are a quicker way for customers to get a taxi than waiting for dispatch to send one from the depot."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxiTutorialStand]"
msgstr "Place a Taxi Stand"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TaxiTutorialStand]"
msgstr "**Place Taxi Stands in locations where you feel customers might need them most.**\r\n\r\nPlacing a stand will automatically create space on the side of the road for taxis to park there to wait for customers.\r\n\r\n**When a stand is in place, the depot will send out taxis to the stand to wait for customers.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TaxiTutorialStandTrigger]"
msgstr "Place a Taxi Stand"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainGroup]"
msgstr "Trains"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainTutorial]"
msgstr "Trains"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Train services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TrainTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Trains come in two types, one for passengers and another for cargo.**\r\n\r\nBoth can use Outside Connections wherever they are available. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainTutorialRailYard]"
msgstr "Rail Yards"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TrainTutorialRailYard]"
msgstr "**Both passenger and cargo trains require a Rail Yard as a depot.**\r\n\r\nCargo Harbors and Airports also have the option of building an upgrade that allows cargo trains to transport cargo directly to and from them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainTutorialPlaceRailYard]"
msgstr "Build a Rail Yard"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TrainTutorialPlaceRailYard]"
msgstr "**To get started with the train service, first build a Rail Yard.**\r\n\r\nMake sure to have plenty of free space around the yard so that you can build your tracks without any tight corners."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TrainTutorialPlaceRailYardTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Rail Yard"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerTrainTutorial]"
msgstr "Passenger Trains"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerTrainTutorialTrainStation]"
msgstr "Passenger Train Service"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerTrainTutorialTrainStation]"
msgstr "**To get started with the Passenger train service, you will need Train Stations.**\r\n\r\nInside the city, passenger trains use Train Stations as stops. These are where passengers can get on and off the trains."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerTrainTutorialPlaceTrainStation]"
msgstr "Build a Train Station"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerTrainTutorialPlaceTrainStation]"
msgstr "Place your Train Station at a convenient distance from where people live and work.\r\n\r\nAlso ensure that the station has good access to the road network, so citizens can easily arrive at the station.\r\n\r\n**Leave plenty of room on both ends of the station** so you can build train tracks with gentle, sloping curves.\r\n\r\n**For service within the city, build at least 2 stations.**\r\n\r\nFor train service to the outside world you'll only need 1 station, but check your map first if you have access to tracks going to an outside connection."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[PassengerTrainTutorialPlaceTrainStationTrigger]"
msgstr "Build Train Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerTrainTutorialTracks]"
msgstr "Train Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerTrainTutorialTracks]"
msgstr "For trains to be able to run from station to station, the stations need to be connected by tracks. \r\n\r\n**Choose the type of train track you'd like to use.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerTrainTutorialCurveTool]"
msgstr "Curve Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerTrainTutorialCurveTool]"
msgstr "When building tracks, remember that trains cannot make tight turns due to their length. \r\n\r\n**To create smooth bends without abrupt corners, use the Curve tool option.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerTrainTutorialBuildTrainTracks]"
msgstr "Building Train Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerTrainTutorialBuildTrainTracks]"
msgstr "**Now that you have your stations, connect them to each other with Train Tracks.**\r\n\r\nRemember to also connect the rail network to the Rail Yard so that trains dispatched from the yard can reach the stations.\r\n\r\nIf you're aiming for an outside connection, build a connection to the tracks leading to the outside connection."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[PassengerTrainTutorialBuildTrainTracksTrigger]"
msgstr "Build Train Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerTrainTutorialLineTool]"
msgstr "Passenger Railway Line Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerTrainTutorialLineTool]"
msgstr "Passenger Railway Line Tool **is used to connect existing stations to create a railway line.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerTrainTutorialRailwayLine]"
msgstr "Making a Railway Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerTrainTutorialRailwayLine]"
msgstr "**Select each Train Station in the order you'd like the train service to run, making a loop.**\r\n\r\nMake sure trains have access to the platform you have selected, also check that the tracks allow trains to return back to the beginning station.\r\n\r\n**Complete the loop by selecting the station platform where you started from.**\r\n\r\nWhen you have completed the loop,your line is complete and trains will automatically be sent out from the Rail Yard and begin operating the line.\r\n\r\nIf you have access to an outside connection, it can be added to the line as a station."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[PassengerTrainTutorialRailwayLineTrigger]"
msgstr "Make a Railway Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainOutsideTutorial]"
msgstr "Passenger Railway Line Outside Connection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Railway Line to Other Cities"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainOutsideTutorialAccess]"
msgstr "Access to an Outside Connection Point"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TrainOutsideTutorialAccess]"
msgstr "**Check to see if the map already has existing tracks that are connected to an Outside Connection Point, and that you own a Map Tile with part of that track in it.**\r\n\r\n**If Yes,** you only need to connect your tracks to the ones leading outside to enable train traffic to and from the outside world.\r\n\r\n**if No,** you will need to buy map tiles to reach either a part of the track leading to the outside connection, or all the way to the edge of the map where the connection point is.\r\n\r\nAs with all tracks, make sure the connection is a smooth curve."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TrainOutsideTutorialAccessTrigger]"
msgstr "Build train tracks to connect with the existing tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrainOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "Connecting a Passenger Railway Line to Outside"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TrainOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "You will need to have at least one Train Station built in your city.\r\n\r\n**With the Passenger Railway Line Tool, select one or more stations and the Outside Connection Point.**\r\n\r\n**Complete the line by selecting the first station again, creating a loop.**\r\n\r\nKeep an eye on what platforms you have selected, and that trains from those platforms can run to the outside connection.\r\n\r\nOnce finished, trains will automatically be sent out from the Rail Yard and begin operating the line."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TrainOutsideTutorialConnectTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect a Passenger Railway Line to an Outside Connection Point"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainTutorial]"
msgstr "Cargo Trains"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainTutorialTrainTerminal]"
msgstr "Cargo Train Terminals"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainTutorialTrainTerminal]"
msgstr "**To get started with the Cargo train service, you will need Cargo Train Terminals.**\r\n\r\nCargo trains use Cargo Train Terminals to load and unload cargo."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainTutorialPlacingTerminal]"
msgstr "Building a Cargo Train Terminal"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainTutorialPlacingTerminal]"
msgstr "**Choose a location for the terminal that is close to industrial areas, where materials are needed.**\r\n\r\nAlso ensure that the terminal has good access to the road network, so trucks can easily bring in and take away cargo.\r\n\r\n**Make sure there is plenty of room around the terminal** for you to build train tracks with gentle, sloping curves."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoTrainTutorialPlacingTerminalTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Cargo Terminal"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainTutorialTracks]"
msgstr "Train Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainTutorialTracks]"
msgstr "For trains to be able to run from terminal to terminal, the terminals need to be connected by tracks. \r\n\r\n**Choose the type of train track you'd like to use.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoTrainTutorialTracksTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect your Terminals to Form a Loop"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainTutorialCurveTool]"
msgstr "Curve Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainTutorialCurveTool]"
msgstr "When building tracks, remember that trains can't make tight turns due to their length. \r\n\r\n**To create smooth bends without abrupt corners, use the Curve Tool Option.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainTutorialBuildTracks]"
msgstr "Building Train Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainTutorialBuildTracks]"
msgstr "**Now that you have your terminals, connect them to each other with Train Tracks.**\r\n\r\nTake advantage of elevation and build tunnels or elevated tracks in already populated areas.\r\n\r\nRemember to also connect the rail network to your Rail Yard so that trains can reach the terminals."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoTrainTutorialBuildTracksTrigger]"
msgstr "Build Train Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainTutorialRouteTool]"
msgstr "Cargo Route Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainTutorialRouteTool]"
msgstr "The Cargo Railway Route Tool **is used to create routes that run from one terminal to the next.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainTutorialMakeRoute]"
msgstr "Making Cargo Routes"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainTutorialMakeRoute]"
msgstr "**Select each Cargo Train Terminal in the order you would like the cargo route to run, making a loop.**\r\n\r\n**Complete the loop by selecting the first terminal again.**\r\n\r\nWhen you have completed the loop, your route is done and trains will automatically be sent out from the Rail Yard and begin operating the route."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoTrainTutorialMakeRouteTrigger]"
msgstr "Make a Cargo Route"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainOutsideTutorial]"
msgstr "Cargo Railway Route Outside Connection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Cargo trains to other cities"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "To enable cheaper and more efficient delivery of goods and materials in and out of the city, **you can create Cargo Railway Routes to the outside world.**\r\n\r\nUsing cargo trains for import and export lowers the number of delivery trucks on the highways, alleviating traffic jams."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoTrainOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "Connecting to Outside Connection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoTrainOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "You will need to have at least one Cargo Train Terminal built in your city.\r\n\r\nWith the Cargo Railway Route Tool, **select one or more terminals and the Outside Connection Point.**\r\n\r\n**Complete the route by selecting the first terminal again, creating a loop.**\r\n\r\nOnce finished, trains will automatically be sent out from the Rail Yard and begin operating the route, delivering goods and materials to and from the terminals. Delivery trucks will then handle their further distribution."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoTrainOutsideTutorialConnectTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect Cargo Route"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorial]"
msgstr "Tram"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Trams"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Trams are a handy way of transporting a large number of citizens around the city. \r\n\r\n**Tram tracks can be flexibly built on top of existing streets as well as standalone networks.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialTramDepot]"
msgstr "Tram Depot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialTramDepot]"
msgstr "**To start a tram line you'll first need a Tram Depot.**\r\n\r\nThe depot houses and maintains a fleet of trams. More can be built as the city and citizen needs grow."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialTramDepotPlace]"
msgstr "Building a Tram Depot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialTramDepotPlace]"
msgstr "**Place the Tram Depot next to a road that is large enough to easily accommodate tracks.**\r\n\r\nMake sure the intersection can handle traffic and the trams coming in and out of the Depot."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TramTutorialTramDepotPlaceTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Tram Depot"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialOneWayTrack]"
msgstr "One-Way Tram Track"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialOneWayTrack]"
msgstr "**The one-way track has one set of rails, with trams traveling in one direction.**\r\n\r\nUseful if you need to navigate through narrow spaces."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialDoubleTrack]"
msgstr "Double Tram Track"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialDoubleTrack]"
msgstr "**The double track has two sets of rails running in opposite directions.**\r\n\r\nIt's a handy way of building a two-way tram network in just one go."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialPlaceTracks]"
msgstr "Build  Tram Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialPlaceTracks]"
msgstr "**Build Tram Tracks to places where citizens would like to go.**\r\n\r\nTrams can navigate corners better than trains, but can still run into trouble if the track has curves that are too tight. \r\n\r\nYou can get creative with the tool modes and build tracks that run in tunnels, or overpasses.\r\n\r\nIf you want the tracks to run on an existing road, use the Replace tool while you have the track selected.\r\n\r\n**Remember to have the tracks connected to the Tram Depot.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TramTutorialPlaceTracksTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a set of Tram Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialTramStops]"
msgstr "Tram Stops"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialTramStops]"
msgstr "Tram Stops are where trams **pick up and drop off passengers.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialPlaceStops]"
msgstr "Place Tram Stops"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialPlaceStops]"
msgstr "**Stops can be placed on either side of the tracks,** so pay attention when setting them up and planning your tram lines.\r\n\r\nPlace stops in places that are convenient for citizens to get on and off and where you think they benefit the city best."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TramTutorialPlaceStopsTrigger]"
msgstr "Place at least two stops"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialTramLine]"
msgstr "Tram Line Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialTramLine]"
msgstr "Tram Line Tool **is used to connect existing stops to create a tram line.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TramTutorialPlaceTramLine]"
msgstr "Creating a Tram Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TramTutorialPlaceTramLine]"
msgstr "**With the Tram Line Tool, select Tram Stops in the order you would like the line to run in, making a loop**\r\n\r\n**To complete the Tram Line, select the first stop again to close the loop.**\r\n\r\nYou can build overlapping lines where the same Tram Stop services many Tram Lines.\r\n\r\nThe Tram Depot does not need to be part of the line. It's enough to have tracks running past it. Trams will start operating automatically once you create your first Tram Line."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TramTutorialPlaceTramLineTrigger]"
msgstr "Make a Tram Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorial]"
msgstr "Subway"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Subway"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Subway trains are a great way to build public transportation in a busy city.**\r\n\r\nUnderground tunnels for Subways can be built anywhere without obstructing other traffic."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialYard]"
msgstr "Subway Yard"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialYard]"
msgstr "To get started with subways, **first build a Subway Yard,** which will serve as a depot for the trains."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialPlaceYard]"
msgstr "Building a Subway Yard"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialPlaceYard]"
msgstr "**Find a large enough open area,** somewhere not too far from the area where you plan to build your subway line.\r\n\r\n**Make sure to have free space on both ends of the yard** so you have enough room to build track connections for Subway Trains to come in and go out.\r\n\r\n**Note** that even though subway tracks run underground, there will need to be enough room on the ends of the subway yard tracks to make a low slope upwards or downwards."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[SubwayTutorialPlaceYardTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Subway Yard"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialUndergroundStation]"
msgstr "Underground Subway Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialUndergroundStation]"
msgstr "The underground station is a handy way to provide passengers access to the subway.\r\n\r\nThe station entrance only takes a small space above ground, while **most of the station and the tracks are located 20 meters below the surface.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialOvergroundStation]"
msgstr "Overground Subway Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialOvergroundStation]"
msgstr "The overground station is the cheaper way to provide passengers access to the subway.\r\n\r\nThe station and tracks are placed on the surface, so they **take up more space. You'll need to also leave room for tracks that lead underground.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialPlaceStations]"
msgstr "Build Subway Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialPlaceStations]"
msgstr "Subway Stations are where passengers can get on and off the trains. Place them in locations where you feel they serve the passengers best.\r\n\r\n**You will need at least 2 subway stations to start a subway line.**\r\n\r\nMake sure to have enough room to slope the tracks up or down as needed."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[SubwayTutorialPlaceStationsTrigger]"
msgstr "Build at least 2 Subway Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialDoubleTrack]"
msgstr "Double Subway Track"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialDoubleTrack]"
msgstr "**The double track has two sets of rails running in opposite directions.**\r\n\r\nIt's a handy way of building a two-way subway network in just one go."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialOneWayTrack]"
msgstr "One-Way Subway Track"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialOneWayTrack]"
msgstr "**The one-way track has a single set of rails, with trains traveling in one direction.**\r\n\r\nUseful if you want to build one-way routes, or build two directions but keep them separate from each other."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialBuildTracks]"
msgstr "Build Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialBuildTracks]"
msgstr "**Now that you have your stations placed, connect them with Subway Tracks.**\r\n\r\n**Remember to also build tracks to connect the Subway Yard to the rest of the track network.**\r\n\r\nSubway tracks can run on various elevation levels, so you can build several lines that cross each other underground. You can also build tracks overground to cross over roads and buildings. \r\n\r\nWhen building tracks, remember that trains cannot make tight turns due to their length. To create smooth bends, use the Curve tool option."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[SubwayTutorialBuildTracksTrigger]"
msgstr "Build Subway Tracks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialLineTool]"
msgstr "Subway Line Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialLineTool]"
msgstr "Subway Line Tool **is used to connect existing stations to create a subway line.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SubwayTutorialConnectLine]"
msgstr "Connecting Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SubwayTutorialConnectLine]"
msgstr "Use the Subway Line Tool to create a line between your stations.\r\n\r\n**Select the stations in the order you want the line to run, include all stations that you want to be part of the line and complete the loop by selecting the station you started with.**\r\n\r\nOnce the line is complete, subway trains will start operating automatically. You can build new stations, tracks, and lines as needed."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[SubwayTutorialConnectLineTrigger]"
msgstr "Create a Subway Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WaterTransportationGroup]"
msgstr "Water"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SeawaysTutorial]"
msgstr "Seaways"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SeawaysTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Ships"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SeawaysTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Ships are able to carry a large number of passengers and huge amounts of cargo.**\r\n\r\nThey are a good way to establish tourist routes and cheaper transportation for goods and materials."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SeawaysTutorialSeawaySizes]"
msgstr "Seaway"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SeawaysTutorialSeawaySizes]"
msgstr "**Seaways are lanes assigned on the water marking the area where ships are allowed to travel.**\r\n\r\nSeaways have ship traffic going both directions. The wider the lane, the more ships are able to use simultaneously."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerShipsTutorial]"
msgstr "Passenger Ships"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerShipsTutorialHarbor]"
msgstr "Harbor"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerShipsTutorialHarbor]"
msgstr "**Passenger ships operate from Harbors.** \r\n\r\nPassenger ships can carry a large number of passengers from one Harbor to another as well as to and from Outside Connections."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerShipsTutorialPlaceHarbor]"
msgstr "Build a Harbor"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerShipsTutorialPlaceHarbor]"
msgstr "**Find a suitable place on the shoreline, with enough room on the water to fit the Harbor.**\r\n\r\nLook around and check how the existing waterways are shaped and where they lead.\r\n\r\nShips might not fit through narrow passages, or under low bridges.\r\n\r\nHarbors will also need a road connection so passengers have access to it.\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[PassengerShipsTutorialPlaceHarborTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Harbor"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerShipsTutorialPlaceSeaway]"
msgstr "Build a Seaway"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerShipsTutorialPlaceSeaway]"
msgstr "**Choose the size of the seaway to build with.**\r\n\r\nYou can connect different sizes to each other and replace existing ones at any time.\r\n\r\nIf you want to connect to places further away, you'll need to buy enough map tiles with waterways connecting to where you want to go.\r\n\r\n**Note:**When planning your route, you might need to replace low bridges with higher ones to let ships pass under them.\r\n\r\nIf you're aiming for an outside connection, you can connect to a ready-built seaway if part of it is in a map tile you own."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[PassengerShipsTutorialPlaceSeawayTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Seaway"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerShipsTutorialPassangerLine]"
msgstr "Passenger Ship Line Tool **is used to place waypoints for Passenger Ships to follow.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerShipsTutorialConnectingLine]"
msgstr "Make a Passenger Ship Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerShipsTutorialConnectingLine]"
msgstr "**With the Passenger Ship Line Tool, select a Ship Dock in your Harbor, and place waypoints to create a line where you want the ships to go.**\r\n\r\n**Loop back to the Ship Dock you started from to complete the route.**\r\n\r\nIf you have more than one Harbor, you can include them in the line, people can then use the ships as means of traveling.\r\n\r\nYou can also build a sightseeing line by creating a loop with only waypoints in it.\r\n\r\n**Note:** You can add new waypoints and harbors after the line is complete."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[PassengerShipsTutorialConnectingLineTrigger]"
msgstr "Create a Passenger Ship Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ShipOutsideTutorial]"
msgstr "Cruise Line Outside Connection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ShipOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Cruise Lines to other cities"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ShipOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Cruises are not just fun but useful. Passenger Ships coming in from neighboring cities can carry tourist and people moving into the city.\r\n\r\n**For some fun and taking the pressure off of roads, you can make a Passenger Ship Line to the Outside World.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ShipOutsideTutorialAccess]"
msgstr "Access to an Outside Seaway"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ShipOutsideTutorialAccess]"
msgstr "**Check to see if the map has an existing seaway that connects to an Outside Connection Point.**\r\n\r\n**If you do:**You don't need to own all the tiles to the Outside Connection Point, as long as a part of the existing seaway is in a tile you own, you can connect to it.\r\n\r\n**If you don't:** you'll need to buy map tiles to reach the Outside Connection point and build a seaway to it.\r\n\r\n**Note:** make sure that there are no low bridges on the way, and that there is clear access from the connection point to your harbor."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ShipOutsideTutorialAccessTrigger]"
msgstr "Build a Seaway to Connect with the Outside Connection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ShipOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "Connecting a Passenger Ship Line to the Outside"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ShipOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "You will need to have at least one Harbor in your city.\r\n\r\n**With the Passenger Ship Line Tool select one or more Harbors and the Ship Outside Connection point.**\r\n\r\n**Complete the line by selecting the first Harbor again, making a loop.**\r\n\r\nWhen you have completed the line Passenger Ships will start operating the line, bringing in tourist and new citizens."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ShipOutsideTutorialConnectTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect a Passenger Ship Line to an Outside Connection Point"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoShipsTutorial]"
msgstr "Cargo Ships"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoShipsTutorialHarbor]"
msgstr "Cargo Harbor"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoShipsTutorialHarbor]"
msgstr "**Cargo ships operate from Cargo Harbors.**\r\n\r\nCargo ships can carry immense amounts of cargo aboard just one ship. \r\n\r\nThey are a great way to handle import and export when you have access to an Outside Connection by water."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoShipsTutorialPlaceHarbor]"
msgstr "Build a Cargo Harbor"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoShipsTutorialPlaceHarbor]"
msgstr "**Find a suitable place on the shoreline, with enough room on the water to fit the harbor area.**\r\n\r\nLook around to see where the waterways lead, ships might not fit through very narrow passages.\r\n\r\nRemember that the harbour will also need a road connection, so trucks can arrive to load and unload cargo.\r\n\r\n**Note:** You can build the road to go along the front of the harbour building after you have built it, and if you are having trouble getting the road to match, try moving the building.\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoShipsTutorialPlaceHarborTrigger]"
msgstr "Place a Cargo Harbor"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoShipsTutorialRoutes]"
msgstr "Cargo Ship Route Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoShipsTutorialRoutes]"
msgstr "Cargo Ship Route Tool **is used to place waypoints for cargo ships to follow.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoShipsTutorialConnectingRoutes]"
msgstr "Make a Cargo Ship Route"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoShipsTutorialConnectingRoutes]"
msgstr "**With the Cargo Ship Route Tool, select a Cargo Ship Dock in your Harbor, and place waypoints to create a line where you want the ships to go.**\r\n\r\n**Loop back to the Cargo Dock you started from to complete the route.**\r\n\r\nIf you have more than one Cargo Harbor, you can select them as a destination, or build routes to Outside Connection points where you can.\r\n\r\n**Note:**You can add new waypoints and move the existing ones after the route is complete."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoShipsTutorialConnectingRouteTrigger]"
msgstr "Create a Cargo Ship Route"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoShipOutsideTutorial]"
msgstr "Shipping Route Outside Connection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoShipOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Shipping Routes to other cities"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoShipOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Trucks carrying goods and materials can create lots of noise and traffic jams.\r\n\r\n**For cheaper and more efficient transportation of goods, you can create a Cargo Ship Route to neighboring cities.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoShipOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "Connecting Shipping Routes to the Outside"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoShipOutsideTutorialConnect]"
msgstr "You will need to have at least one Cargo Harbor in your city.\r\n\r\n**With the Cargo Ship Route Tool select one or more Cargo Harbors and the Ship Outside Connection.**\r\n\r\n**Complete the route by selecting the first Cargo Harbor again, making a loop.**\r\n\r\nWhen you have completed the route, Cargo Ships will start operating the route, transporting goods and materials."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoShipOutsideTutorialConnectTrigger]"
msgstr "Connect a Cargo Ship Route to an Outside Connection Point"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AirTransportGroup]"
msgstr "Air"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AirwaysTutorial]"
msgstr "Airways"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AirwaysTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Airways"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[AirwaysTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Airplanes can carry a large number of passengers or great amounts of cargo** from one place to another very quickly."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AirwaysTutorialAirport]"
msgstr "Airport"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[AirwaysTutorialAirport]"
msgstr "To get started and get airplanes flying, **you'll need to build an Airport.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AirwaysTutorialPlaceAirport]"
msgstr "Build an Airport"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[AirwaysTutorialPlaceAirportTrigger]"
msgstr "Build an Airport"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerAirlineTutorial]"
msgstr "Passenger Airlines"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerAirlineTutorialLine]"
msgstr "Passenger Airplane Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerAirlineTutorialLine]"
msgstr "Passenger Airplane Line Tool is used to connect Airports to each other and **give airplanes a route to follow.**\r\n\r\nPassenger lines between neighboring cities carry tourists and people moving in.\r\n\r\nLarge cities with more than one airport can have Passenger Lines flying within the city."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PassengerAirlineTutorialConnectLine]"
msgstr "Connecting Passenger Air Lines"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PassengerAirlineTutorialConnectLine]"
msgstr "**With the Passenger Airplane Line Tool, select a Passenger Airplane Terminal at the Airport.**\r\n\r\n**Then select the Terminals and Outside Connections you want to include in the Line, in the order you want the Line to operate in.**\r\n\r\nFor Outside Connections locate and select the Outside Connection marker somewhere on the edge of the map.\r\n\r\nFor flights within the city, select a Passenger Terminal at the airport you want the line to fly to.\r\n\r\nYou can select as many airports and outside connections as you like.\r\n\r\n**Complete the line by selecting the Passenger Airplane Terminal where you started from.**\r\n\r\n**Note:** You can add more airports to the line and move the existing ones after the line is complete.  Terminals and Outside Connections can have more than one line operating out of them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[PassengerAirlineTutorialConnectLineTrigger]"
msgstr "Create a Passenger Airplane Line"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoAirlineTutorial]"
msgstr "Cargo Airlines"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoAirlineTutorialCargoRoute]"
msgstr "Cargo Airplane Route"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoAirlineTutorialCargoRoute]"
msgstr "Cargo Airplane Route Tool is used to connect Airports to each other and **give airplanes a route to follow.**\r\n\r\nCargo Routes between neighboring cities carry goods and materials to and from the city.\r\n\r\nLarge cities with more than one airport can have Cargo Routes flying within the city. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CargoAirlineTutorialConnectingRoute]"
msgstr "Connecting Cargo Air Routes"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CargoAirlineTutorialConnectingRoute]"
msgstr "To get Cargo Routes started **you'll need to build a Cargo Terminal Upgrade to your Airport.**\r\n\r\nSelect your airport and find the upgrade in the Upgrades panel.\r\n\r\n**Then, with the Cargo Airplane Route Tool, select a Cargo Airplane Terminal in your new cargo extension of the Airport.**\r\n\r\n**Select cargo terminals in airports you'd like to include in the route, in the order you want the Cargo Route to operate in.**\r\n\r\nFor Outside Connections, locate and select the outside connection marker somewhere on the edge of the map.\r\n\r\nFor flights within the city, select a Cargo Terminal at the airport you want the line to fly to.\r\n\r\nYou can select as many airports and outside connections as you like.\r\n\r\n**Complete the line by selecting the Cargo Airplane Terminal where you started from, to make a loop.**\r\n\r\n**Note:** You can add more airports to the route and move the existing ones after the route is complete. Terminals and Outside Connections can have more than one route operating out of them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CargoAirlineTutorialConnectingRouteTrigger]"
msgstr "Create a Cargo Airplane Route"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[Parks&RecreationGroup]"
msgstr "PARKS & RECREATION"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ParksTutorial]"
msgstr "Parks & Recreation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ParksTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Parks & Recreation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ParksTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Recreation buildings, areas and parks increase the attractiveness of your city.**\r\n\r\nHaving an attractive city adds to the well-being of citizens and promotes tourism."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ParksTutorialMaintanence]"
msgstr "Parks Maintenance"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ParksTutorialMaintanence]"
msgstr "To increase the variety and size of the parks available, **unlock the Park Maintenance Depot in the Progression panel.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TouristAttractionsTutorial]"
msgstr "Tourist Attractions"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TouristAttractionsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Tourism"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TouristAttractionsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Tourism is a great way to generate more income for the city.**\r\n\r\nVisiting tourists bring in money by buying things in shops, eating out, and by staying in hotels, for example."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LandmarksTutorial]"
msgstr "Landmarks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LandmarksTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Landmarks"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LandscapingGroup]"
msgstr "LANDSCAPING"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TerraformingTutorial]"
msgstr "Terraforming"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TerraformingTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Terraforming"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TerraformingTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**The ground can be shaped** to build gardens, pastures, sanctuaries,  reservoirs, flood channels and more."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TerraformingTutorialTools]"
msgstr "Tools"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TerraformingTutorialTools]"
msgstr "There are a variety of different ground shaping tools.\r\n\r\n**Check each tool's description for more information.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TerraformingTutorialBrush]"
msgstr "Brush"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TerraformingTutorialBrush]"
msgstr "**Increase or decrease the brush size** for large scale work or detailed shaping.\r\n\r\nChange the **Brush Strength to control how much the terrain changes** with each application."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[VegetationTutorial]"
msgstr "Vegetation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[VegetationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Vegetation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[VegetationTutorialGlobalTheme]"
msgstr "Theme"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[VegetationTutorialGlobalTheme]"
msgstr "**Plants and trees are regional,** so a different set is available based on the currently selected theme."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[VegetationTutorialToolMode]"
msgstr "Tool Mode"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[VegetationTutorialToolMode]"
msgstr "You can **place plants individually** or use a **brush to distribute larger amounts at once.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PathwaysTutorial]"
msgstr "Pathways"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PathwaysTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Pathways"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PathwaysTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Pathways are for pedestrians and can be built wherever there is room.**\r\n\r\nWith pathways, you can build nature trails or scenic routes between buildings. Connect your path to a road's sidewalk so that people can access it."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PathwaysTutorialTools]"
msgstr "Tool Options"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PathwaysTutorialTools]"
msgstr "Tool options help you bring your vision to life. Get creative with curves and elevations."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CommunicationGroup]"
msgstr "COMMUNICATIONS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PostServiceTutorial]"
msgstr "Post"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PostServiceTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Postal Services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PostServiceTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The postal service delivers different types of mail around the city. Mail is an important form of communication for citizens and companies.\r\n\r\n**A well working Post Service will have a positive effect on citizen well-being and the operating efficiency of companies.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PostServiceTutorialPostOffice]"
msgstr "Post Office"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PostServiceTutorialPostOffice]"
msgstr "To get mail delivery going in your city, first build a Post Office.\r\n\r\n**Post Offices handle local mail to and from the residents and local businesses.**\r\n\r\nResidents can visit the office directly to drop off their mail for delivery. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PostServiceTutorialMailBox]"
msgstr "Mail Boxes"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PostServiceTutorialMailBox]"
msgstr "**Mail Boxes can be placed by the side of any road.** They are used by citizens to drop off mail for delivery.\r\n\r\nPlace them in convenient locations around residential areas and places people frequent.\r\n\r\nPost Vans will automatically collect mail from the Mail Boxes."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TelecomServiceTutorial]"
msgstr "Telecom Service"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TelecomServiceTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Basic Services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TelecomServiceTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Citizens automatically have access to basic radio, TV, and Internet.** \r\n\r\nMore advanced services can be built to better the lives of the citizens."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TelecomServiceTutorialAdvanced]"
msgstr "Advanced Wireless Services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TelecomServiceTutorialAdvanced]"
msgstr "**Telecommunications service buildings provide services like mobile networks and wireless broadband.**\r\n\r\nBuilding Advanced Communication Services to better communications, improve citizen well-being, help Commercial zone sales, and decrease the material costs for Industrial zones."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityCategory]"
msgstr "CITY"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MapTileGroup]"
msgstr "MAP TILES"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MapTileTutorial]"
msgstr "Map Tiles"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MapTileTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Map Tiles"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MapTileTutorialNaturalResources]"
msgstr "Natural Resources"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MapTileTutorialNaturalResources]"
msgstr "You can plan out your city's industrial development by expanding to **areas that offer one or more of the natural resources the industry needs.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MapTileTutorialPurchasing]"
msgstr "Purchasing"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MapTileTutorialPurchasing]"
msgstr "**To buy new map tiles, you need both money and expansion permits.**\r\n \r\nExpansion permits are granted whenever you reach a new milestone."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OutsideConnectionTutorial]"
msgstr "Outside Connections"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OutsideConnectionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Outside Connections"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[OutsideConnectionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Outside Connections are routes to neighboring cities and the rest of the outside world. \r\n\r\n**They can be roads and tracks as well as water or air routes.**\r\n\r\nThey are **used by people moving in as well as visiting tourists.**\r\n\r\nYour **city also imports and exports goods and materials** through these connections."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OutsideConnectionTutorialMakingConnections]"
msgstr "Road and Track Connections"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[OutsideConnectionTutorialMakingConnections]"
msgstr "To build a new Outside Connection you need to **expand your city by buying map tiles until you reach the edge of the map.**\r\n\r\nOnce done, **build roads or tracks right up to the edge, and an Outside Connection Point will be established.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OutsideConnectionTutorialWaterRoutes]"
msgstr "Water Routes"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[OutsideConnectionTutorialWaterRoutes]"
msgstr "Connections to the outside world over water **can be created when you have a Seaway that reaches the edge of the map.**\r\n\r\nYou don't need to own all the tiles up to the edge if there is a pre-built Seaway that has a ship-type Outside Connection Point. Buy enough tiles to connect your Seaway to the pre-built one.\r\n\r\nIf no pre-built Seaway exists, you need to buy all the tiles to reach the map edge."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OutsideConnectionTutorialAirRoutes]"
msgstr "Air Routes"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[OutsideConnectionTutorialAirRoutes]"
msgstr "**Air connections to the outside world can be created without buying map tiles.**\r\n\r\nPassenger lines and cargo routes can be created directly from an Airport to an airplane-type Outside Connection Point on the map edge to establish the connection."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ProgressionGroup]"
msgstr "PROGRESSION"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DevelopmentTutorial]"
msgstr "Development"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DevelopmentTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Development Trees"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DevelopmentTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Development trees allow you to access new service buildings and other buildable items for your city.** \r\n\r\nYou can unlock these assets by using development points."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DevelopmentTutorialNewFeatures]"
msgstr "New Features"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DevelopmentTutorialNewFeatures]"
msgstr "**Basic Services will be automatically unlocked when you reach new milestones.**\r\n\r\nTo develop and grow the service further, unlock the next item along the tree and build it in your city.\r\n\r\n**Development points are awarded for reaching new milestones.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DevelopmentTutorialServicesTrees]"
msgstr "Services Development Trees"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DevelopmentTutorialServicesTrees]"
msgstr "**Each City Service has its own development tree.**\r\n\r\nYou can focus on specific types of city development by spending your points in the areas that interest you the most.\r\n\r\nExplore the development trees to find what features each service has to offer."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MilestoneTutorial]"
msgstr "Milestones"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MilestoneTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Milestones"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MilestoneTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Milestones represent the growth of your city.**\r\n\r\nDevelop your city to reach the next milestone."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MilestoneTutorialCityGrowth]"
msgstr "City Growth"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MilestoneTutorialXP]"
msgstr "XP"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MilestoneTutorialXP]"
msgstr "This tracks the amount of Expansion Points (XP) you have, and shows how much you need to gain until you reach the next milestone.\r\n\r\nYou are awarded **XP for building City Service buildings and their upgrades, Signature Buildings, and roads.**\r\n\r\n**Increasing your population numbers and citizen happiness** also grants you XP rewards."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MilestoneTutorialMoney]"
msgstr "Money"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MilestoneTutorialMoney]"
msgstr "**Whenever you reach a new milestone, you will receive a money reward.**\r\n\r\nUse it to build and maintain the city."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MilestoneTutorialDevPoints]"
msgstr "Development Points"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MilestoneTutorialDevPoints]"
msgstr "For each milestone reached you will also be granted development points, which **can be used to unlock advanced services.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MilestoneTutorialExpPermits]"
msgstr "Expansion Permits"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MilestoneTutorialExpPermits]"
msgstr "Each milestone adds to your existing expansion permits. \r\n**They are needed to expand the city borders.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MilestoneTutorialUnlockedFeatures]"
msgstr "Unlocked Features"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MilestoneTutorialUnlockedFeatures]"
msgstr "**Each milestone unlocks more features** that allow you to build and expand your city in different ways.\r\n\r\nYou can look through the list here."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityInfoTutorial]"
msgstr "CITY INFORMATION"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityInfoTutorialDemand]"
msgstr "Demand"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CityInfoTutorialDemand]"
msgstr "Different types of zones are in demand in your city at different times.\r\n\r\nAs people move in and businesses develop, the need for new zoning changes according to their needs.\r\n\r\n**The fuller the bar, the higher the demand for the zone type is.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityInfoTutorialDemandPlusMinus]"
msgstr "Plus and Minus"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityInfoTutorialCityPolicies]"
msgstr "City Policies"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CityInfoTutorialCityPolicies]"
msgstr "You can shape the rules and regulations of your city by enacting city policies.\r\n \r\n**These policies affect the entire city, including all districts and buildings.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityInfoTutorialCityPoliciesActivate]"
msgstr "Activating City Policies"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CityInfoTutorialCityPoliciesActivate]"
msgstr "**You can activate and deactivate policies any time.**\r\n\r\nSome policies have adjustment sliders to fine-tune their effect.\r\n\r\nSelect a policy to see what its effects are.\r\n\r\nMore policies are unlocked as you reach certain milestones."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningGroup]"
msgstr "ZONES"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorial]"
msgstr "Zoning Residential"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Zones"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Zoning determines which types of buildings are built in the selected area.**\r\n\r\nBuildings in zoned areas **get built automatically** depending on the city's needs.\r\n\r\nAreas that are not zoned will remain empty."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialZoneTypes]"
msgstr "Zone Types"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningTutorialZoneTypes]"
msgstr "**Zones are divided into groups according to the types of buildings they allow for.**\r\n\r\nThis is your way to allot different areas of the city for different uses. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialGlobalTheme]"
msgstr "Theme"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningTutorialGlobalTheme]"
msgstr "You can choose which theme the zoned buildings follow.\r\n\r\n**This affects the visual style of buildings that get built in an area.**\r\n\r\nYou can change the theme at any time. The change will come into effect for any new zoning you apply."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialToolMode]"
msgstr "Tool Modes"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningTutorialToolMode]"
msgstr "**Different modes help you shape your zones exactly the way you want.**\r\n\r\nKeep in mind that for buildings to get built, the zoned area needs to be adjacent to a road and have enough space on it."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ResidentialZoningTutorialLowDensity]"
msgstr "Low Density Housing"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ResidentialZoningTutorialLowDensity]"
msgstr "This zone type is for **single family homes.**\r\n\r\nThey have a positive effect on the residents' happiness, but can become expensive to live in when land value rises. \r\n\r\nDifferent job types and the availability of jobs affect the demand for different types of housing."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ResidentialZoningTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "Zoning for Residential"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ResidentialZoningTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "**Zoning an area for residential use will allow people to start moving into your city.**\r\n\r\nFor a happier neighborhood, place your residential areas in suitable locations. People appreciate living away from sources of pollution such as industry and large, noisy highways.\r\n\r\nYou are free to design your residential areas in any way you like. When building roads, you can make use of the grid tool mode to create bigger neighborhoods in an instant, or you can take your time and plan scenic, winding roads along a shoreline. \r\n\r\n**Whatever the design, make sure the area is connected to the rest of your city and the outside world.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ResidentialZoningTutorialPlaceTrigger]"
msgstr "Place a Low Density Housing Zone"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CommercialZoningTutorial]"
msgstr "Zoning Commercial"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CommercialZoningTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Commercial Zones"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CommercialZoningTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Commercial zones provide space for different types of business uses.**\r\n\r\nBusinesses provide services and jobs for the residents and increase city income by paying taxes and fees."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CommercialZoningTutorialLowDensity]"
msgstr "Low Density Business"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CommercialZoningTutorialLowDensity]"
msgstr "**This zone type is for single story or low-rise buildings for small business.**\r\n\r\nThese businesses provide important local services such as stores, restaurants and gas stations for citizens living nearby."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CommercialZoningTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "Zoning for Commercial"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CommercialZoningTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "**Zoning land for commercial space will allow for commercial companies to start up businesses.**\r\n\r\nFor a prospering business area, place it close to where the people are. Close proximity to residential zones benefits both the residents and the businesses.\r\n\r\nA good balance between these two will increase citizen happiness and provide both services as well as jobs.\r\n\r\n**Businesses will continually need new goods and materials. Having good connections to larger roads nearby makes for faster and easier deliveries.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CommercialZoningTutorialPlaceTrigger]"
msgstr "Place a Commercial Zone"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[IndustrialZoningTutorial]"
msgstr "Zoning Industrial"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[IndustrialZoningTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Industrial Zones"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[IndustrialZoningTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Industrial zones provide land for industrial businesses.**\r\n\r\nIndustries provide jobs and increase the city's tax revenue."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[IndustrialZoningTutorialManufacturing]"
msgstr "Industrial Manufacturing"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[IndustrialZoningTutorialManufacturing]"
msgstr "**This zone type allows for all types of industrial manufacturing buildings and warehouses.**\r\n\r\nIndustrial companies process materials to create material goods, which can be further processed by other industries, sold by commercial businesses, or used by city services."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[IndustrialZoningTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "Zoning for Industrial Manufacturing"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[IndustrialZoningTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "**Zoning land for industrial use will allow industrial companies to start up businesses.**\r\n\r\nIndustrial companies are dependent on materials, so they work best near resources and benefit greatly from good traffic connections in their immediate vicinity.\r\n\r\n**Manufacturing creates pollution, so it's best to place these zones away from people's homes and groundwater deposits.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[IndustrialZoningTutorialPlaceTrigger]"
msgstr "Place an Industrial Zone"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OfficeZoningTutorial]"
msgstr "Zoning Office"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OfficeZoningTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Office Zones"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[OfficeZoningTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Office zones house companies that produce immaterial goods and services.**\r\n\r\nServices such as software and telecommunications for private citizens and other companies."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OfficeZoningTutorialLowDensity]"
msgstr "Low Density Office"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[OfficeZoningTutorialLowDensity]"
msgstr "The Low Density Office zone allows for **single story or low-rise buildings for small business.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[OfficeZoningTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "Zoning for Office"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[OfficeZoningTutorialPlace]"
msgstr "**Zoning land for office space will allow for businesses creating immaterial goods to start moving in.**\r\n\r\nOffices will continually need new resources, but as these are immaterial goods, physical connections such as large roads are not as important for offices as they are for other zones.\r\n\r\n**Offices will, however, require skilled employees, so having a well-built education system is beneficial.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[OfficeZoningTutorialPlaceTrigger]"
msgstr "Place an Office Zone"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorial]"
msgstr "Specialized Industry"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Specialized Industry"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Specialized Industry areas can be built wherever relevant natural resources are available.**\r\n \r\nCompanies operating the areas will gather the resources and transport them to be either used within the city or exported outside."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialAttraction]"
msgstr "Attracting Industries"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialAttraction]"
msgstr "**Active specialized areas will make the city more attractive to industries that can make use of those specific materials.**\r\n\r\nThis means that you will start to see more related industries appearing in industrial zones."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialHighlight]"
msgstr "Highlighting Resources"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialHighlight]"
msgstr "**When you select a specialization type, related natural resources are highlighted on the map.**\r\n\r\nYou can also turn the highlights for different resources on and off in the related Info View."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialType]"
msgstr "Available Resources"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialType]"
msgstr "Choose the types of specialization according to the **types of resources you have available on your map.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialPlacement]"
msgstr "Build a Hub Building "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialPlacement]"
msgstr "Place the hub building close to the relevant resource.\r\n\r\n**The circle around the hub building shows how far from the building you can extend the area.**\r\n\r\nRemember that your hub building needs a road connection in the front of the building, and that you can't build roads inside the area."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialPlacementTrigger]"
msgstr "Place an Extractor Building Area"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialArea]"
msgstr "Define the Area"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialArea]"
msgstr "**Define the active area starting from one edge of the building. Place nodes in the shape you want, and complete the area by selecting the opposite edge of the building.**\r\n\r\nYou can adjust the active area with the Area Tool at any time you need to.\r\n\r\nWhen the area is completed, a company will move in and start production."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[SpecialIndustryZoneTutorialAreaTrigger]"
msgstr "Define the active area for the Specialized Industry"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningDemandTutorial]"
msgstr "Zoning Demand"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningDemandTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Zoning Demand"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningDemandTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "These bars give you at-a-glance info on what types of zones are currently needed in your city. \r\n\r\n**The fuller the bar, the higher the demand is for that zone type.**\r\n\r\nEach bar grows and shrinks constantly according to the city's changing needs  and your zoning efforts."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialRemoveZone]"
msgstr "Removing and Rezoning"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialRemoveZoneIntro]"
msgstr "Removing Zones"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningTutorialRemoveZoneIntro]"
msgstr "If you ever feel that a zone is in the wrong place, you can choose to remove the entire zone, or shape it by removing parts of it.\r\n\r\nUse the Tool Mode of your choice and, **while holding down** <inputAction:Tool/Secondary Apply> **paint the area you want to remove.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningTutorialRemoveZoneIntroGamepad]"
msgstr "If you ever feel that a zone is in the wrong place, you can choose to remove the entire zone, or shape it by removing parts of it.\r\n\r\nUse the Tool Mode of your choice and, **while holding down**<inputAction:Tool/Secondary Apply> **paint the area** you want to remove."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialRemoveZoneRezoning]"
msgstr "Rezoning"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningTutorialRemoveZoneRezoning]"
msgstr "If you would like to change an already zoned area to a different type, **you can apply new zoning on top of the old one.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialRemoveZoneCondemned]"
msgstr "Condemned Buildings"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ZoningTutorialRemoveZoneCondemned]"
msgstr "**Buildings that already exist in the area you are rezoning or removing zoning from will become condemned.** \r\n\r\nCondemned buildings will get automatically demolished over time.\r\n\r\nFor a faster rebuild time, you can bulldoze them yourself to make room for new buildings."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AreasGroup]"
msgstr "AREAS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorial]"
msgstr "Districts"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Districts"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DistrictTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "You can create different types of neighborhoods or specialize an area for a specific use with the district tool.\r\n\r\n**Districts let you assign policies and city services to only be in effect inside the district.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorialDistrict]"
msgstr "District Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DistrictTutorialDistrict]"
msgstr "With the district tool you can **create and adjust the borders of a district.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorialSnapping]"
msgstr "Snapping"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DistrictTutorialSnapping]"
msgstr "Different tool snapping modes can help you shape the area.\r\n\r\n**Turn them on or off one by one or use the star button to toggle them all at once.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorialCreation]"
msgstr "Creating a District"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DistrictTutorialCreation]"
msgstr "With the District Creation Tool selected, **create the district border by placing nodes with** <inputAction:Tool/Apply>\r\n\r\n**Complete the area by selecting the node you started with.**\r\n\r\nYou can reshape the completed area with the District Creation Tool at any time by either dragging the existing nodes to new locations or by placing new nodes anywhere on the border line."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[DistrictTutorialCreationTrigger]"
msgstr "Create a District"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorialInfo]"
msgstr "District Information"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DistrictTutorialInfo]"
msgstr "**First close the Areas Tool Menu and then open the district information panel by selecting the name of the district you created.**\r\n\r\nThe panel contains all the relevant information about the district. The amount of information shown will vary according to what is going on inside the district.\r\n\r\nHovering over the items in the panel will give you more information.\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[DistrictTutorialInfoTrigger]"
msgstr "Select District Name"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorialName]"
msgstr "District Name"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DistrictTutorialName]"
msgstr "You can **change the name** of the district by selecting the name and typing in a new one.\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorialPolicies]"
msgstr "District Policies"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DistrictTutorialPolicies]"
msgstr "**Here you can assign policies that will be in effect inside the district.**\r\n\r\nSee the tooltip for each policy for more information.\r\n\r\nSelecting the icon will toggle the policy on and off."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DistrictTutorialDelete]"
msgstr "Delete District"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DistrictTutorialDelete]"
msgstr "**Deleting a district will remove the area boundaries and the policies that were in effect inside the district.**\r\n\r\nServices assigned to the district will reset to city-wide services.\r\n\r\nRemoving a district will not affect zoned buildings, roads, service buildings or any landscaping you might have done inside the area."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecializationAreaTutorial]"
msgstr "Industrial Specialization Area Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SpecializationAreaTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Area Tool"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SpecializationAreaTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**You can adjust any Specialized Industry area after it has been created.**\r\n\r\nYou can select and move any existing node to adjust the shape or create a new node by selecting the line between existing nodes."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SignatureBuildingTutorial]"
msgstr "SIGNATURE BUILDINGS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SignatureBuildingTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Signature Buildings"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SignatureBuildingTutorialEffects]"
msgstr "Positive Effects"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SignatureBuildingTutorialEffects]"
msgstr "**Many Signature Buildings have a positive effect on the area surrounding it.**\r\n\r\nThese effects and more information about each building are shown when you hover over the building."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SignatureBuildingTutorialTypes]"
msgstr "Types of Signature Buildings"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SignatureBuildingTutorialTypes]"
msgstr "Signature Buildings **are organized by the zones** they are related to in the menu, **but they can be placed in any zone** on the map."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SignatureBuildingTutorialTheme]"
msgstr "Theme"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SignatureBuildingTutorialTheme]"
msgstr "**Each theme has its own corresponding set of signature buildings.**\r\n\r\nYou can build one of each from both sets."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EconomyGroup]"
msgstr "CITY ECONOMICS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BudgetTutorial]"
msgstr "Budget"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BudgetTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Budget"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BudgetTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**The budget tab allows you to view your financial situation at a glance.**\r\n\r\nThe numbers are constantly updated according to changes taking place in the city.\r\n\r\n**By selecting a revenue or expense item from the list, you can see further information about it.**\r\n\r\nBreakdown of the selected item is displayed on the right side panel. At the same time, the item's share of the total is highlighted on the bar graph on the left. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BudgetTutorialRevenues]"
msgstr "Revenues"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BudgetTutorialRevenues]"
msgstr "The revenues list shows you the different **sources and amounts of money coming into the city.** "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BudgetTutorialExpenses]"
msgstr "Expenses"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BudgetTutorialExpenses]"
msgstr "The expenses list shows you the different **payments made by the city.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BudgetTutorialBalance]"
msgstr "Monthly Balance"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BudgetTutorialBalance]"
msgstr "The monthly balance displays **how much profit or loss your city will generate each month** based on the current revenue and expense values."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LoanTutorial]"
msgstr "Loans"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LoanTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Loans"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LoanTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Loans are a great way to finance city building when needed.\r\n\r\nWhen taking out a loan pay attention to the interest rate and the resulting monthly cost so you don't end up overspending.\r\n\r\n**When you have borrowed money, you are only paying the interest every month.**\r\n\r\nIn other words, the loaned amount remains the same until you choose to pay it back."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LoanTutorialAdjusting]"
msgstr "Adjusting The Loan Amount"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LoanTutorialAdjusting]"
msgstr "**To take out a loan, move the slider to the right until you reach the desired amount.**\r\n\r\nThe loan interest rate and resulting monthly cost are adjusted automatically.\r\n\r\nYou have an upper limit to how much you can borrow. This increases as you reach milestones."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LoanTutorialAccepting]"
msgstr "Accepting The Loan"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LoanTutorialAccepting]"
msgstr "**When you have the desired amount in the Total Loan Amount window, select Accept.**\r\n\r\nAfter accepting the loan your city immediately receives the funds and you can spend them however you like.\r\n\r\nIf you need to borrow more money in the future,  move the slider further right and select Accept again. The new loan is combined with the old one, so in effect you only ever have one active loan and one monthly payment."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxationTutorial]"
msgstr "Taxation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Taxation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TaxationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The taxation tab allows you to **view and adjust the tax rates** and the resulting revenues or expenses of individual zone types."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxationTutorialType]"
msgstr "Type"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TaxationTutorialType]"
msgstr "**You can adjust the Tax Rate to an entire Zone Type with the sliders.**\r\n\r\nFor more precise control, expand a zone type to adjust tax rates based on individual citizen or resource type."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxationTutorialTaxRate]"
msgstr "Tax Rate"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TaxationTutorialTaxRate]"
msgstr "**Increasing or decreasing taxes can be beneficial to your city depending on your situation.**\r\n\r\nSetting a negative tax rate means the city pays subsidies. This can be used to attract citizens or businesses into the city."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TaxationTutorialEstimate]"
msgstr "Estimate"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TaxationTutorialEstimate]"
msgstr "**This is a projected estimation for monthly tax income based on the current situation.**\r\n\r\nThe total is adjusted constantly according to changes taking place in the city, such as residents moving in and out and businesses being established or closed down."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ServicesTutorial]"
msgstr "Services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ServicesTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Services"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ServicesTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**The services tab shows the current revenues and expenses related to running your city services.**\r\n\r\nIt also allows you to adjust service budgets and related fees to fine-tune your economy. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ServicesTutorialSelection]"
msgstr "Service Selection"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ServicesTutorialSelection]"
msgstr "Select a city service to view the revenues and expenses, and to adjust the budget for the service. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ServicesTutorialBudget]"
msgstr "Budget"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ServicesTutorialBudget]"
msgstr "**Lowering the budget saves money, but the quality of the service also goes down.**\r\n\r\nA higher budget results in better service quality, but will cost more.\r\n\r\nSome services also charge a fee for their use. Higher fees result in more revenue, but will have a negative impact on citizen happiness and business profitability."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ProductionTutorial]"
msgstr "Production"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ProductionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Production"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ProductionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The production tab shows you how much your city is producing.\r\n\r\n**You can view production from materials, material goods, and immaterial goods.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ProductionTutorialMaterials]"
msgstr "Materials"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ProductionTutorialMaterials]"
msgstr "**Expand the categories and hover over a material to see the production chain on the right panel for that material.**\r\n\r\nThe production chain shows where materials and goods come from and what they are used for, as well as a visual indication of whether you're currently importing or exporting them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TransportationOverviewTutorial]"
msgstr "TRANSPORTATION OVERVIEW"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TransportationOverviewTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Transportation Overview"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TransportationOverviewTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**All transportation lines and routes you have built so far are shown here.**\r\n\r\nThey are divided into the categories of Public Transport Lines and Cargo Routes.\r\n\r\nYou can view and adjust each line and route separately."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TransportationOverviewTutorialTypes]"
msgstr "Transport types"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TransportationOverviewTutorialTypes]"
msgstr "Find the right lines and routes by selecting the **type of transportation vehicle** they are related to."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityStatisticsTutorial]"
msgstr "CITY STATISTICS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityStatisticsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "City Statistics"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CityStatisticsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**The statistics panel shows you detailed graphs about the progress of your city.**\r\n\r\nSeeing whether a selected topic is trending up or down can help you steer your city in the direction you want.\r\n\r\n**For example,** if you would like your city to be visited by lots of tourists, and the number of visiting tourists is in decline, you could focus on building more attractions and making sure there are enough hotels for them to stay in. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityStatisticsTutorialToggles]"
msgstr "Toggling Statistic Views"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CityStatisticsTutorialToggles]"
msgstr "**Select an aspect of the city you would like to see or hide by turning it on or off. The relevant graphs will be shown on the right.**\r\n\r\nSome categories can be expanded to show further options."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CityStatisticsTutorialTimeScale]"
msgstr "Time Scale"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CityStatisticsTutorialTimeScale]"
msgstr "**Slide the scale** to view the displayed graph in a **different time frame.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UpgradeTutorial]"
msgstr "BUILDING UPGRADES"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UpgradeTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Upgrading Service Buildings"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[UpgradeTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Upgrades provide things such as higher capacity or faster and cleaner services.**\r\n\r\nUpgrades are specific to each service building. \r\n\r\n**Select a service building you have built to see what upgrades are available.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UpgradeTutorialUpgrading]"
msgstr "Building an Upgrade"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[InfoViewsCategory]"
msgstr "INFO VIEWS & NOTIFICATIONS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[InfoViewGroup]"
msgstr "INFO VIEWS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[InfoviewTutorial]"
msgstr "Info Views"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[InfoviewTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Info Views"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[InfoviewTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Info Views display useful information as a visual overlay.**\r\n\r\nThese include things such as traffic flow, natural resource locations, wind speed, water flow, and so on.\r\n\r\nYou can also see statistics about City Service coverage, Citizen Happiness, and more.\r\n\r\n**Selecting a service will also highlight the locations of the City Service Buildings and Service Vehicles.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TourismTutorial]"
msgstr "Tourism"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TourismTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Tourism"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TourismTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Tourism can be an **important source of income for the city and employs a lot of people.** "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TourismTutorialAttractivenessValue]"
msgstr "Attractiveness Value"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TourismTutorialAttractivenessValue]"
msgstr "**The Attractiveness value reflects how interesting your city is for tourists.**\r\n\r\n It can be increased by building special attractions."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TourismTutorialTouristGo]"
msgstr "**Tourists spend their time and money on different types of activities.**\r\n\r\nYou can find them going to places that provide relaxation, visiting attractions, going shopping, doing leisure activities, and staying at hotels."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TourismTutorialAttractions]"
msgstr "Attractions"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TourismTutorialTerrain]"
msgstr "Attractive Terrain"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TourismTutorialTerrain]"
msgstr "**Some terrain features have a further positive effect on the attractiveness value of the buildings in their vicinity.**\r\n\r\nFeatures such as sea shores, forests, mountains and special features like ruins."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorial]"
msgstr "Outside Connections"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Outside Connection Points"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Outside Connections are routes to and from the world outside the map.**\r\n\r\nYour most important Outside Connection is the highway. It allows for new citizens to move in, for tourists to visit the city, and for the import and export of resources, goods, and even some City Services. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialMap]"
msgstr "Map Connections"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialMap]"
msgstr "**All maps come with at least one road-based connection to the outside world, but as soon as you unlock enough tiles to reach the edge of the map, you can build new ones.**\r\n\r\nNew connections can be roads, railways or seaways.\r\n\r\nAn Outside Connection Point is created automatically when the traffic network in question is extended to the edge of the map."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialTrade]"
msgstr "Trading"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialTrade]"
msgstr "**Trading is the importing and exporting of goods and services.**\r\n\r\nCity Services available for import or export are Electricity, Water & Sewage, Road Maintenance, Healthcare, Garbage Management, Fire & Rescue, Police, and Education."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialElectricityTrade]"
msgstr "Electricity Trade"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialElectricityTrade]"
msgstr "Electricity trade is made possible by the map's existing Power Line connection.\r\n\r\n**To start trading, connect a power station to the Power Line connection.** \r\n\r\nMore connections can be built as desired."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialWaterTrade]"
msgstr "Water and Sewage Trade"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LocateOutsideConnectionsTutorialWaterTrade]"
msgstr "Water & Sewage Service can also be opened for trading.\r\n\r\n**To start trading, build water and sewage pipes from the city to the map edge.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BuildingLevelTutorial]"
msgstr "Building Level"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BuildingLevelTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Building Levels"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BuildingLevelTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Each zoned building has a level between 1-5. \r\n\r\n**The level affects the functionality and rent of the building.**\r\n\r\nRent is used to pay for building upkeep, and any remaining money is used for improving the building, i.e. leveling up. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BuildingLevelTutorialEfficiency]"
msgstr "Building Efficiency"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BuildingLevelTutorialEfficiency]"
msgstr "**Higher level buildings consume less and produce less waste.**\r\n\r\nConsumption of water and electricity per household or per resource produced or sold is less.\r\n\r\nThere is less garbage and pollution produced by the building.\r\n\r\nAlso higher level industrial buildings have a more efficient output."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BuildingLevelTutorialStyle]"
msgstr "Building Level Style"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BuildingLevelTutorialStyle]"
msgstr "**Higher level buildings are also visually distinct** from their lower level counterparts."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LandValueTutorial]"
msgstr "Land Value"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LandValueTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Land Value"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LandValueTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Land Value is a tool you can use to find out where citizens wish to live and where companies want to set up their businesses.**\r\n\r\nNearby City Service Buildings will increase Land Value, but only if the citizens find the services useful. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[LandValueTutorialWealth]"
msgstr "Wealth and Land Value"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LandValueTutorialWealth]"
msgstr "**Wealthier citizens and profitable companies are willing to move to areas of higher Land Value.**\r\n\r\nHigher Land Value also means the area is more costly, so poorer citizens and companies will gravitate towards areas of lower Land Value."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CompanyProfitabilityTutorial]"
msgstr "Company Profitability"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CompanyProfitabilityTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Company Profitability"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CompanyProfitabilityTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Company profitability is based on how much money it has left after paying its running costs.**\r\n\r\nCompanies use their income and wealth to level up their buildings, which increases their efficiency."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CompanyProfitabilityTutorialWorkers]"
msgstr "Number of Employees"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CompanyProfitabilityTutorialWorkers]"
msgstr "**Companies attempt to maximize their income. This means they adjust the number of employees according to how well the company is doing.**\r\n\r\nIf a company’s income and wealth are both negative, the company will go bankrupt."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[NaturalResourcesTutorial]"
msgstr "Natural Resources"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[NaturalResourcesTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Find Natural Resources"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[NaturalResourcesTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Different types of natural resources are available based on the map you are building your city on.** \r\n\r\nResources are highlighted on the map in different colors."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[NaturalResourcesTutorialIndustry]"
msgstr "Specialized Industry Areas"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[NaturalResourcesTutorialIndustry]"
msgstr "**These areas can be placed on natural resources to benefit from them. Choose the type of industry according to the resources available.**\r\n\r\nHaving specialized industries using the resources also leads to your city having more industrial manufacturing that relate to the resource. "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PopulationTutorial]"
msgstr "Population"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PopulationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Population View"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PopulationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The population view shows a number of different **statistics regarding your citizens.**\r\n\r\nThe numbers give a breakdown of how the city is doing in terms of **births and deaths, people moving in and out, and the status of employment.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PopulationTutorialDemographic]"
msgstr "Age Demographic"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PopulationTutorialDemographic]"
msgstr "**The age demographic of your population can be used to guide city development.**\r\n\r\n**For example,** children and seniors cannot work jobs. Only adults can reach the highest education levels, and the elderly require more healthcare.\r\n\r\nThe heat maps can be used to plan which City Services to build and where they would be most beneficial."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[HappinessTutorial]"
msgstr "Happiness"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[HappinessTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Happiness"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[HappinessTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**This view shows a quick breakdown of the types of things your citizens like and dislike in your current city.**\r\n\r\nPay special attention to the negative factors. These show you what your city is currently lacking or where problems lie."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorial]"
msgstr "Workplace Availability"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Workplace Availability"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Workplaces are supplied by companies and City Service buildings, which require varying numbers of employees to function.**\r\n\r\nJobs are divided into levels that match education levels.\r\n\r\nAn employee can work any job that is at or below their education level."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorialPositions]"
msgstr "Citizens Looking for Jobs"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorialPositions]"
msgstr "**Citizens evaluate open positions based on the job level, distance from their home, and the number of already existing employees in the building.**\r\n\r\nThey prefer jobs that match their education level, are closer to home, and have fewer employees."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorialUnemployed]"
msgstr "Citizens Choosing Jobs"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorialUnemployed]"
msgstr "**If a citizen feels that there are no good jobs available, they can also choose to stay unemployed, hoping to find something better later on.**\r\n\r\nEmployed citizens will also occasionally compare their current job to open positions, and change jobs if they find a better one."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorialOpenJobs]"
msgstr "Jobs and Education Level"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[WorkplaceAvailabilityTutorialOpenJobs]"
msgstr "If you find there are plenty of open jobs at a particular education level, consider building more education services that match that level."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CitizenWealthTutorial]"
msgstr "Citizen Wealth"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CitizenWealthTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Citizen Wealth"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CitizenWealthTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Citizens have varying amounts of money on hand depending on their wage, household rent and other expenses."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CitizenWealthTutorialHeatmap]"
msgstr "Areas by Wealth"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CitizenWealthTutorialHeatmap]"
msgstr "**The heatmap is useful for gaining a general idea of the wealth of your citizens as well as pinpointing areas where citizens might be struggling.**\r\n\r\nLowering taxes and building Welfare Offices can be used to help struggling citizens."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[NotificationsGroup]"
msgstr "NOTIFICATIONS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[NotificationTutorial]"
msgstr "Notifications"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[NotificationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Notifications"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[NotificationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Notification icons are shown to highlight different situations.**\r\n\r\nSome guide you in placing buildings and building roads. Others are signs of a problem in an area or a building.\r\n\r\n**Select the icon to see more information.**\r\n\r\nOnce the situation is cleared, the notification also disappears."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadRequiredTutorial]"
msgstr "Road Required"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadRequiredTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "A City Service building has no road access"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadRequiredTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Your **service buildings need to be situated next to a road** for them to receive electricity, and for materials and employees to be able to move in and out."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadRequiredTutorialBuildRoad]"
msgstr "Build a road for Service Building"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadRequiredTutorialBuildRoad]"
msgstr "You can fix the issue by **building a road along the front of the building** and connecting that road to the existing road network.\r\n\r\nYou can locate the front of the building by looking for the Road Required notification icon."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadRequiredTutorialRelocate]"
msgstr "Relocate a Service Building"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadRequiredTutorialRelocate]"
msgstr "You can also solve the issue by relocating the building next to an existing road.\r\n\r\n**Selecting the building opens the building information panel. Select the relocate icon at the bottom,** and you can move the building to a new location."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrafficAccidentTutorial]"
msgstr "Traffic Accident"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TrafficAccidentTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "There has been a traffic accident"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TrafficAccidentTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Help is on the way! \r\n\r\n**The Police, Fire & Rescue, and Healthcare service buildings send out vehicles to the site as needed to handle the situation.**\r\n\r\nTo make accident clearance as efficient as possible, make sure your city has these services available and that police cars, fire engines and ambulances have a clear path to the accident site."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[TrafficAccidentTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to the Fire & Rescue Info View"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AirPollutionTutorial]"
msgstr "Air Pollution"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[AirPollutionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The air is getting polluted"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[AirPollutionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Air Pollution is affecting the Health of citizens.**\r\n\r\nAir Pollution is created mainly by industrial zones, some City Service buildings, and by traffic. It spreads across the map in the air, affected by wind and weather, but fades away quickly once its source is removed. \r\n\r\n**Find polluting City Service buildings and consider upgrading them with filtering systems where you can, or relocate the buildings. Existing industrial zones can be recreated in locations that are further away from people's homes.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[AirPollutionTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to the Air Pollution Info View"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GroundPollutionTutorial]"
msgstr "Ground Pollution"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GroundPollutionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The ground is getting polluted"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[GroundPollutionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Ground Pollution is affecting the Health of citizens.**\r\n\r\nGround pollution is created mainly by industrial buildings and some City Service buildings. It spreads on the ground close to its source, and the main danger it poses is contaminating groundwater. If any part of a groundwater deposit becomes affected by Ground Pollution, all of the water within becomes contaminated. If the contaminated water is then pumped into the city, citizens consuming it will become sick.\r\n\r\n**The pollution in groundwater deposits will eventually fade away once its source is removed.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[GroundPollutionTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to the Ground Pollution Info View"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[NoisePollutionTutorial]"
msgstr "Noise Pollution"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[NoisePollutionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "There is too much noise"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[NoisePollutionTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Noise Pollution is affecting the wellbeing of citizens.**\r\n\r\nNoise Pollution is created by vehicles, some City Service buildings, and abandoned buildings containing squatters.\r\n\r\nNoise pollution is a temporary effect, so it doesn't accumulate, but the effects from multiple sources do stack.\r\n\r\n**Noise pollution is instantly removed when the source is removed.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[NoisePollutionTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to the Noise Pollution Info View"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DirtyWaterTutorial]"
msgstr "Water Pollution"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[DirtyWaterTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The water supply is getting polluted"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[DirtyWaterTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Polluted water in the water network is making citizens sick.**\r\n\r\nLook for a sewage outlet that might be overflowing or a water pumping station that might be pumping from a polluted water source.\r\n\r\n**The pollution in the water supply will eventually fade away once its source is removed.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[DirtyWaterTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to Water Pollution Info View"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MedicalClinicTutorial]"
msgstr "Medical Clinic required"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MedicalClinicTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "A Medical Clinic is needed"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MedicalClinicTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Citizens are getting sick and there's nowhere to treat them. \r\n\r\n**Build a Medical Clinic!**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[MedicalClinicTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to the Healthcare Service"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CemeteryTutorial]"
msgstr "Cemetery required"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CemeteryTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "A Cemetery is needed"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CemeteryTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Citizens have passed away, but there is no place to bury them. \r\n\r\n**Build a Cemetery!**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[CemeteryTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to the Deathcare Service"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FireTutorial]"
msgstr "Building Fire"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FireTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "A building is on fire"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[FireTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Fires in buildings are a danger both to the buildings themselves as well as the residents inside. \r\n\r\n**Don't worry, fire engines have been dispatched to put it out.**\r\n\r\nHowever, if the response time of the Fire & Rescue service is too long, the fire can begin to spread to nearby buildings. The longer the fire goes on, the higher the probability of spreading becomes.\r\n\r\n**Check that your city has the required services available and that the service vehicles have a clear path to the accident site.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[FireTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to the Fire & Rescue Service"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ForestFireTutorial]"
msgstr "Forest Fires"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ForestFireTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The forest is on fire"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ForestFireTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Fires in a forest destroy the wood resources as trees burn down. The fire can spread rapidly, and if there are buildings next to the burning trees, they may catch fire as well.\r\n\r\n**Extinguishing forest fires requires firefighting helicopters,** since fire engines cannot reach areas outside of the road network.\r\n\r\nIf the fire reaches a road, fire engines can help in controlling the fire and keep it from spreading further. \r\n\r\n**Firewatch Towers work as an early warning system** and will help to alert the Fire & Rescue services as soon as a forest fire breaks out in their vicinity.\r\n\r\n**Make sure your city has all the necessary services available.**\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[ForestFireTutorialIntroTrigger]"
msgstr "Navigate to the Fire & Rescue Service"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CitizensCategory]"
msgstr "CITIZENS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ChirperTutorial]"
msgstr "CHIRPER"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ChirperTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Chirps"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ChirperTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Citizens, city services and companies chirp about their situations and opinions.**\r\n\r\nChirps are **often about  problems and successes** in the city and reflect how your citizens are feeling. \r\n\r\nYou can use this to guide your city development decisions."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ChirperTutorialChirperInfo]"
msgstr "Chirper Information"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ChirperTutorialChirperInfo]"
msgstr "Selecting the **chirp writer's name** will open a panel with more **information** about them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FollowingCitizensTutorial]"
msgstr "FOLLOWING CITIZENS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FollowingCitizensTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Following Citizens"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[FollowingCitizensTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "You can choose to follow citizens and get updates on their lives.\r\n\r\nAdding them to this panel makes it easy to check up on them whenever you want."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[FollowingCitizensTutorialFollowNow]"
msgstr "Follow a Citizen"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[FollowingCitizensTutorialFollowNow]"
msgstr "**Move around the city and find someone to follow.**\r\n\r\nSelecting is easier when the simulation is paused.\r\n\r\nWhen you select a citizen, their information panel opens.\r\n\r\n**Find the Follow button in the bottom of the citizen information panel, and select it to add the citizen to your list.**\r\n\r\nYou can use the list later to find them anywhere in the city as well as see any events that have taken place in their life."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EventJournalTutorial]"
msgstr "EVENT JOURNAL"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EventJournalTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Events"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EventJournalTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "The Event Journal is a running collection of events that have taken place in the city.\r\n\r\nThese are things such as **accidents, fires, storms, and celebrations.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EventJournalTutorialFiresAndAccidents]"
msgstr "Fires and Accidents"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EventJournalTutorialFiresAndAccidents]"
msgstr "If you find that the number of **road accidents is high,**  check the condition of your roads and **build Road Maintenance Depots as needed.**\r\n\r\nWhen there is a **high number of building fires** it can be an indication that the **Fire & Rescue services in your city are insufficient.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EventJournalTutorialNaturalDisasters]"
msgstr "Natural Disasters"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EventJournalTutorialNaturalDisasters]"
msgstr "If you're seeing **lots of citizens hurt by natural disasters,** it might be beneficial to **build more Emergency Shelters,** so citizens can take cover when needed."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EventJournalTutorialCelebrations]"
msgstr "Celebrations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EventJournalTutorialCelebrations]"
msgstr "Celebration events show you how many people took part in the event."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RadioTutorial]"
msgstr "RADIO"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RadioTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Radio"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RadioTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Choose the tunes to listen to."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RadioTutorialNetwork]"
msgstr "Broadcasting Network"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RadioTutorialNetwork]"
msgstr "**Radio Networks** are listed here.\r\n\r\nEach network has a selection of radio stations to choose from."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RadioTutorialStations]"
msgstr "Stations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RadioTutorialStations]"
msgstr "The **Radio Stations** available in the network are listed here."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RadioTutorialVolume]"
msgstr "Volume"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RadioTutorialVolume]"
msgstr "Adjust the **volume up or down** to your liking."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RadioTutorialAds]"
msgstr "Radio Commercials"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RadioTutorialAds]"
msgstr "Don't like commercials interrupting your music?\r\n\r\nYou can **turn ads on or off** here."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[Interface&ToolsCategory]"
msgstr "INTERFACE & TOOLS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[KeyboardCameraTutorial]"
msgstr "CAMERA CONTROLS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[KeyboardCameraTutorialMove]"
msgstr "Moving the Camera"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[KeyboardCameraTutorialMoveTrigger]"
msgstr "Move the camera with the <inputAction:Camera/Move> keys."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[KeyboardCameraTutorialRotate]"
msgstr "Rotating the Camera"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[KeyboardCameraTutorialZoom]"
msgstr "Zooming with Camera"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[KeyboardCameraTutorialZoomTrigger]"
msgstr "Zoom in and out with <inputAction:Camera/Zoom[1]> and  <inputAction:Camera/Zoom[0]> or by scrolling with the mouse wheel."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GamepadCameraTutorial]"
msgstr "CAMERA CONTROLS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GamepadCameraTutorialMove]"
msgstr "Moving the Camera"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[GamepadCameraTutorialMoveTrigger]"
msgstr "Move the camera with <inputAction:Camera/Move>"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GamepadCameraTutorialRotate]"
msgstr "Rotating the Camera"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[GamepadCameraTutorialRotateTrigger]"
msgstr "Rotate the camera with <inputAction:Camera/Rotate>"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[GamepadCameraTutorialZoom]"
msgstr "Zooming with Camera"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[GamepadCameraTutorialZoomTrigger]"
msgstr "Zoom in and out with <inputAction:Camera/Zoom>"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PauseMenuTutorial]"
msgstr "PAUSE MENU"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PausemenuTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Settings, options and more"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PausemenuTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "You can visit the pause menu at any time. \r\n\r\nTo Save and Load your game.\r\n\r\nUnder Options you will find settings for Graphics, Audio, Tutorials and more.  \r\n\r\n**The game will be paused while the menu is open.**\r\n\r\n"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SimulationSpeedTutorial]"
msgstr "SIMULATION"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SimulationSpeedTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Simulation"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SimulationSpeedTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Pausing the simulation will temporarily stop time.**\r\n\r\nThis allows you to build, plan and find solutions at your own pace. \r\n\r\nOnce you're done, **unpause the game to resume the flow of time** so that life in the city will continue."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[SimulationSpeedTutorialIntroSpeed]"
msgstr "Simulation Speed"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BulldozerTutorial]"
msgstr "BULLDOZER"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[BulldozerTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Bulldozer"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UndergroundTutorial]"
msgstr "UNDERGROUND VIEW"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UndergroundTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Underground view"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[UndergroundTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "You can **turn the underground view on and off** here at any time.\r\n\r\nTurning on the underground view makes it easier to build at different depths and to find connection points.\r\n\r\nYou can also use it while replacing or bulldozing roads and pipes that are built underground."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeGroup]"
msgstr "PHOTO MODE"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorial]"
msgstr "CINEMATIC CAMERA"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Cinematic Camera"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Make a video!**\r\n\r\nMake your camera move through your city, change the time of day as the camera moves and add camera effects.\r\n\r\nCapture the video with your favorite software."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialTimeLine]"
msgstr "Timeline"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialTimeLine]"
msgstr "**This is the Timeline for your video.**\r\n\r\nStart with the marker at the beginning of the timeline."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialCaptureKey]"
msgstr "Capture Key"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialCaptureKey]"
msgstr "**Tracking your camera movement is done by placing Capture Keys to the timeline.**\r\n\r\nEach Capture Key records the place of the camera at the moment you select the Key.\r\n\r\nStart by moving your camera to where you want to start from and add a Capture Key."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialCurves]"
msgstr "Curves"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialCurves]"
msgstr "**Capture Keys will appear here on the curve.**\r\n\r\nThis is the representation of your camera position."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialTimeLineMove]"
msgstr "Moving on the Timeline"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialTimeLineMove]"
msgstr "**Move the Timeline marker forward.**\r\n\r\nthis is the amount of time that elapses between the Capture Keys.\r\n\r\n**Move your camera and add your next Capture Key.**\r\n\r\nRepeat this as many times as you want, to create the camera movement you want."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialAdjustingKeys]"
msgstr "Adjusting Keys"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialAdjustingKeys]"
msgstr "**Capture Keys can be adjusted on the curve.**\r\n\r\nTo adjust and edit your camera position and movement, you can select Capture Keys on the curve and move them."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialDeleteKeys]"
msgstr "Deleting Keys"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialDeleteKeys]"
msgstr "Select the Capture Key you want to remove on the curve, the Capture Key icon will change to show a minus sign.\r\n\r\n**Delete the Capture Key by selecting the Capture Key icon.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialSegmentDuration]"
msgstr "Segment Duration"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialSegmentDuration]"
msgstr "**To view your timeline in a different length of time,** move the slider back and forth here."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialPreview]"
msgstr "Preview "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialPreview]"
msgstr "**To preview your camera movement or view what position your camera is in a CaptureKey,** move the Timeline marker back and forth on the Timeline."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialAddingCameraEffects]"
msgstr "Adding Camera Effects"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialAddingCameraEffects]"
msgstr "**You can use any of the Camera Effects in your video.**\r\n\r\nTurn on the camera option you want by selecting the checkbox, adjust the effect, and **add it to your Timeline by selecting the Capture Key Icon next to the checkbox.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialAdjustingCameraEffects]"
msgstr "Adjusting Camera Effects"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialAdjustingCameraEffects]"
msgstr "**Camera effect Capture Keys will appear here on a curve.**\r\n\r\nTo make changes like moving from day to night during your camera movement.\r\n\r\nSelect and adjust the camera option you want, add the camera capture key at the beginning of the Timeline, move the Timeline marker to when you want to effect to be different and add a Capture Key with different settings."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialPlay]"
msgstr "Play"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialPlay]"
msgstr "**To play the camera movement, select play.**\r\n\r\nThis will show you the camera movement you have made, without any icons or panels on the screen.\r\n\r\nReturn normal view with <inputAction:Navigation/Back> "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialPlayGamepad]"
msgstr "**To play the camera movement, select play.**\r\n\r\nThis will show you the camera movement you have made, without any icons or panels on the screen.\r\n\r\nReturn normal view with <inputAction:Navigation/Back> "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[CinematicCameraTutorialSaveAndLoad]"
msgstr "Save and Load"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CinematicCameraTutorialSaveAndLoad]"
msgstr "Save and Load the Camera Movement and settings you have made."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorial]"
msgstr "PHOTO MODE"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Taking Pictures"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Take a snapshot or spend some time and delve into the art of photography.** \r\n\r\nYou can use the default camera or fine tune it with lots of options."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialCamera]"
msgstr "Camera"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialCamera]"
msgstr "**Build the camera you want.**\r\n\r\nChange the sensor size, Blade Count and more."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialLens]"
msgstr "Lens"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialLens]"
msgstr "**Tune your Camera Lens.**\r\n\r\nVignette, Bloom and others, get the effect you want."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialColor]"
msgstr "Color"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialColor]"
msgstr "**Get the colors just right.**\r\n\r\nBright and saturated, black and white and everything in between, make it happen here."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialWeather]"
msgstr "Weather"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialWeather]"
msgstr "**Move the clouds and color the sky.**\r\n\r\nClouds not in the right place for the perfect shot or the tint of the sky not what you want? You can fix it here."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialEnvironment]"
msgstr "Environment"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialEnvironment]"
msgstr "**Change the time of day.**\r\n\r\nDon't wait around for the right moment in time, choose it here."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialActivateFeature]"
msgstr "Activating Camera Features"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialActivateFeature]"
msgstr "**Select the check box next to each feature to turn it on.**\r\n\r\nTo change the value, hover over the option and drag the arrows left or right with <inputAction:Tool/Apply>\r\n\r\nYou can also enter the value manually, using a keyboard."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialActivateFeatureGamepad]"
msgstr "**Select the check box next to each feature to turn it on.**\r\n\r\nTo change the value of the feature\r\n\r\nMove to the value with <inputAction:Navigation/Move Horizontal>\r\n\r\nActivate the value by selecting it with <inputAction:Tool/Apply>\r\n\r\n**Use keyboard to change the value.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialTakePhoto]"
msgstr "Take a Photo"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialTakePhoto]"
msgstr "Take Photo button **captures a picture without any of the UI elements and saves it to the Screenshots folder.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[PhotoModeTutorialHideUI]"
msgstr "Hide UI"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialHideUI]"
msgstr "**Hide UI button hides all icons, panels and notifications, including the Photo Mode panel.**\r\n\r\nThis is useful for fine tuning your image composition without obstructions to the view.\r\n\r\n**While in Hide UI mode, take a picture with** <inputAction:Shortcuts/Take Photo>\r\n\r\n**Return to normal view with** <inputAction:Tool/Cancel[1]> "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[PhotoModeTutorialHideUIGamepad]"
msgstr "**Hide UI button hides all icons, panels and notifications, including the Photo Mode panel.**\r\n\r\nThis is useful for fine tuning your image composition without obstructions to the view.\r\n\r\nWhile in Hide UI mode, **take a picture with** <inputAction:Shortcuts/Take Photo>\r\n\r\n**Return to normal view with** <inputAction:Navigation/Back>"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.HINTS_TITLE"
msgstr "Hints"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MilestoneTutorialCityGrowth]"
msgstr "Track how your city is growing and how far you have to go to reach the next milestone.\r\n\r\n**Milestones unlock new features, dev points and more.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MapTileTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Purchasing new map tiles expands the area you can build on.**\r\n\r\nYou can select tiles with <inputAction:Tool/Apply> and deselect them with <inputAction:Tool/Secondary Apply> \r\n\r\nTo select or deselect multiple tiles at once, **hold down the button, drag and release.**\r\n\r\nYou can make your city any shape you like. **Tiles don't need to be connected, but it is easier to grow your city if they are.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MapTileTutorialIntroGamepad]"
msgstr "**Purchasing new map tiles expands the area you can build on.**\r\n\r\nYou can select tiles with <inputAction:Tool/Apply> and deselect them with <inputAction:Tool/Secondary Apply>\r\n\r\nTo select or deselect multiple tiles at once, **hold down the button, drag and release.**\r\n\r\nYou can make your city any shape you like. **Tiles don't need to be connected, but it is easier to grow your city if they are.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[AirwaysTutorialPlaceAirport]"
msgstr "Airports are some of the largest service buildings, and need a lot of room.\r\n\r\n**Look for a large, flat area to build your Airport on, and keep an eye on the airport airspace.**\r\n\r\nAirspace is room for airplanes to climb after takeoff and for approaching the airport for landing.\r\n\r\nRemember to also build road access to the Airport so that passengers and cargo can move in and out without problems."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TODO_OUTRO_GAMEPAD"
msgstr "Well Done!\r\n\r\nYou have completed your tasks and now have the basic knowledge of how to build a city.\r\n\r\nGoing forward, more tutorials and hints will pop up to guide you on your way.\r\n\r\nIf you feel like you no longer need the guidance, **you can turn tutorials off in the pause menu.**\r\n\r\nYou can always review old tutorials by accessing the Advisor with <inputAction:Shortcuts/Advisor>.\r\n\r\n\r\n**Would you like to continue building this city or start a new one?**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[KeyboardCameraTutorialRotateTrigger]"
msgstr "Rotate the camera with <inputAction:Camera/Rotate[2]> and <inputAction:Camera/Rotate[3]> \r\nTilt the camera with <inputAction:Camera/Rotate[0]> and <inputAction:Camera/Rotate[1]> \r\nor hold down the middle mouse button and move the mouse."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TrainOutsideTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "To decrease the highway traffic caused by visiting tourist and people moving into the city, **you can create Passenger Railway Lines to the outside world.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[LandmarksTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**Landmarks make the city an attractive destination for tourists, and increases the happiness of citizens.**\r\n\r\nLandmarks are somewhat expensive to upkeep. Place them thoughtfully and keep an eye on their numbers."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[VegetationTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Trees, bushes and other greenery can be used to decorate your city.\r\n\r\n**Trees grows with the passing of time.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[CityInfoTutorialDemandPlusMinus]"
msgstr "**Plus signs indicate an abundance of the described resource or labor force type** in your city, which increases demand for the zone type.\r\n\r\n**Minus signs indicate a lack of the described resource or labor force type** in your city, which lowers demand for the zone type."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SignatureBuildingTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "**These are unique buildings that unlock one by one as you fulfill requirements specific for each building.**\r\n\r\nOnce unlocked, you can build one of each of the Signature Buildings.\r\n\r\nBuilding a Signature Building grants you XP."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[TourismTutorialTouristGo]"
msgstr "Where tourists go"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[TourismTutorialAttractions]"
msgstr "**Attractions are buildings with an Attractiveness value.**\r\n\r\nThese include **Parks & Recreation** buildings, and some **Signature Buildings.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EducationTutorialIntroEducationElementary]"
msgstr "Elementary School"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EducationTutorialIntroEducationElementary]"
msgstr "**Elementary schools provide the beginning of the education path for citizens.**\r\n\r\nElementary Schools provide a passive well-being bonus to nearby families with children."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[EducationTutorialIntroEducationElementaryBuild]"
msgstr "Building an Elementary School"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[EducationTutorialIntroEducationElementaryBuild]"
msgstr "**Build elementary schools in areas with families.**\r\n\r\nChildren will start attending the nearest school to their home, no matter how far it is.\r\n\r\n**Keep in mind that if children need to travel too far to attend elementary school, it will negatively affect their families well-being.**\r\n\r\nSchools can hold a limited amount of students, so you will need to build more schools as the population grows."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TRIGGER[EducationTutorialIntroEducationElementaryBuildTrigger]"
msgstr "Build an Elementary School"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[UpgradeTutorialUpgrading]"
msgstr "Select the upgrade you want from the menu.\r\n\r\n**Depending on the upgrade, it is added to an existing building as an extension, or placed on the side as a separate sub-building.**\r\n\r\nMost upgrades can only be applied once to any given building, but there are some you can add multiple times."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UpgradeTutorialSubBuildingArea]"
msgstr "Sub-Building Area"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[UpgradeTutorialSubBuildingArea]"
msgstr "**Sub-Building Upgrades can be build anywhere within the marked area.**\r\n\r\nSub-Buildings don't need to be connected to a road or be build on the zoning grid, they can be freely placed on any free spot inside the area."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UpgradeTutorialPedestrianAccess]"
msgstr "Pedestrian Access to the Sub-Buildings"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[UpgradeTutorialPedestrianAccess]"
msgstr "**If Sub-Buildings are placed outside the zoning grid or away from the road, you will need to build Paths for Pedestrian Access.**\r\n\r\nYou can get creative and build complex areas for the service building by adding paths, landscaping and other items within the Sub-Building Area."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UpgradeTutorialRelocatingSubBuildings]"
msgstr "Relocating Sub-Buildings"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[UpgradeTutorialRelocatingSubBuildings]"
msgstr "You can move individual Sub-Buildings after they have been build.\r\n\r\n**Select the building and find the relocate icon in the info panel or find the sub-building in the main building panel and select relocate in the dropdown next to the sub-building name you want.**\r\n\r\nYou can also relocate the whole Service Building Complex with its extensions and sub-buildings by selecting the relocate icon in the main service building panel."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[UpgradeTutorialServiceToSubBuildings]"
msgstr "Water and Electricity to the Sub-Buildings"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[UpgradeTutorialServiceToSubBuildings]"
msgstr "Water, Sewage and Electricity to the Sub-Buildings are handled by the main service building.\r\n\r\n**The pipes and electricity lines to the sub-buildings are not visible on the map.** \r\n\r\nOnly the main building needs to be connected to a road for all the sub-buildings to receive the services.  "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[MapTileTutorialMapTileUpkeep]"
msgstr "Map Tile Upkeep Cost"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[MapTileTutorialMapTileUpkeep]"
msgstr "**New map tiles will start to accrue an Upkeep Cost**. The total amount of the upkeep is determined by how much each map tile costs to purchase and how many map tiles you have already purchased. After you have purchased new map tiles, the cost will be added to the city expenses. You can view all your city expenses in the City Economy Budget panel."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[FireAndRescueTutorialFireDamage]"
msgstr "**Buildings that have been damaged but not collapsed will get repaired automatically** and the cost is taken from the building rent.\r\n\r\n**Collapsed buildings are dealt by Disaster Response Unit**. Disaster Response Unit is an upgrade for Fire Stations, that searches for survivors in the collapsed building and clears out the rubble afterwards.\r\n\r\n**Disaster Response Units can be imported from an Outside Connection** when the city can’t provide them.\r\n\r\n**You can always Bulldoze Collapsed Buildings** manually to have the site checked, cleared and ready for rebuilding."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DISABLE_CONFIRMATION_TEXT"
msgstr "Even with tutorials disabled, you can always access the information from the **Advisor.**\r\n\r\nThe Advisor offers helpful information in case you get stuck, or want more in-depth information on a feature.\r\n\r\nSelect the **?** symbol located in the top right corner of the screen to open the Advisor and browse the content.\r\n\r\nIf at some point you would like to resume the tutorials, go to the **pause menu options section to turn them on again.**\r\n\r\n**Are you sure you want to disable tutorials?**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DISABLE_CONFIRMATION_TEXT_GAMEPAD"
msgstr "Even with tutorials disabled, you can always access the information from the **Advisor.**\r\n\r\nThe Advisor offers helpful information in case you get stuck, or want more in-depth information on a feature.\r\n\r\nPress <inputAction:Shortcuts/Advisor> to open the Advisor and browse the content.\r\n\r\nIf at some point you would like to resume the tutorials, go to the **pause menu options section to turn them on again.**\r\n\r\n**Are you sure you want to disable tutorials?**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ConsoleHintsTutorial]"
msgstr "CONTROLLER HINTS"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ConsoleHintsTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Controller Hints"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[ConsoleHintsTutorialIntroGamepad]"
msgstr "The controller actions are shown at the top of the screen."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialElevationStep]"
msgstr "Adjusts **how much the elevation changes** with each step of adjustment."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.LIST_REMINDER_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr "When you're ready, there is a tutorial task waiting for you.\r\n\r\n**Find the next task in the task list to continue learning how to build a city.**"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BulldozerTutorialIntro]"
msgstr "Made a mistake? Don't worry, you can bulldoze it. \r\n\r\nSelect the bulldozer and use\r\n\r\n<inputAction:Tool/Apply> **to remove objects and get a refund.**\r\n\r\n<inputAction:Shortcuts/Bulldozer> is a shortcut to activate the bulldozer at any time."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[BulldozerTutorialIntroGamepad]"
msgstr "Made a mistake? Don't worry, you can bulldoze it. \r\n\r\nSelect the bulldozer and use\r\n\r\n<inputAction:Tool/Apply> **to remove objects and get a refund.**\r\n\r\n<inputAction:Shortcuts/Bulldozer> is a shortcut to activate the bulldozer at any time."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[SimulationSpeedTutorialIntroSpeed]"
msgstr "If you feel time is going by too slowly or passing by too quickly, you can **switch between three different simulation speeds.**\r\n\r\n<inputAction:Shortcuts/Pause Simulation> is a shortcut to pause and unpause the simulation at any time."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialToolOptionsGamepad]"
msgstr "**Hide UI button hides all icons, panels and notifications, including the Photo Mode panel.**\r\n\r\nThis is useful for fine tuning your image composition without obstructions to the view.\r\n\r\n**While in Hide UI mode, take a picture with** <inputAction:Shortcuts/Take Photo>\r\n\r\n**Return to normal view with** <inputAction:Navigation/Back> "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.DESCRIPTION[RoadsTutorialToolOptionSnappingGamepad]"
msgstr "These options can help fine-tune the shape and placement of your roads. \r\n\r\n**With snapping on, roads and other objects become magnetic to what snapping modes you have turned on.**\r\n\r\nSnapping options can be turned on and off individually.\r\n\r\n**Before you start building your roads close the Tool Options menu with** <inputAction:Tool/Cancel> "

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialToolOptionSnappingGamepad]"
msgstr "Snapping"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialToolOptionsGamepad]"
msgstr "Tool Options"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Welcome]"
msgstr "Welcome to the Editor"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TODO_INTRO"
msgstr "To help you get started with the Editor, we've prepared a series of tutorials. These will provide an overview of the Editor's features and show you where to find various controls.\r\n\r\n**When tutorials are turned On, guides will appear as you interact with new panels in the Editor.** You can toggle tutorials On or Off using the ? icon.\r\n\r\nYou can also find all available tutorials in the Advisor panel. Each tutorial can be started from the Advisor, even if tutorials are turned Off.\r\n\r\n**Would you like to continue with the tutorials?**"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Introduction]"
msgstr "Introduction"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Introduction]"
msgstr "**With this editor you can make maps, edit assets and import custom meshes and textures to create new assets.**\r\n\r\nNew maps that are created will be added to the custom map gallery and can then be used in game.\r\n\r\nYou can edit existing assets, and make variants of them to be used in game.\r\n\r\nIf you have made your own models to be used as assets, you can import custom geometry and textures and configure them to be used in game. "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[MenuButtons]"
msgstr "Menu Buttons"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[MenuButtons]"
msgstr "**Buttons for the main actions you can take.** These will open their own panels to the side for more controls."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Simulation]"
msgstr "Simulation"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Simulation]"
msgstr "**You can turn the simulation On and adjust the speed, to test your creation.** \r\n\r\nSee how water flows on your map, how your assets function, and how everything looks under different conditions."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[CalendarAndWeather]"
msgstr "Calendar and Weather"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[CalendarAndWeather]"
msgstr "Adjusting the Time, Calendar and Cloudiness slider gives you a quick look on how they affect your map and assets."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Toggles]"
msgstr "Toggles"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Toggles]"
msgstr "Variety of icons are here for actions like changing the camera mode, turning tutorials On/Off and returning to the Pause Menu."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[InfoViews]"
msgstr "Info Views"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[InfoViews]"
msgstr "**Info Views will provide you a visual of where resources are and how much of them there are.** \r\n\r\nThis is especially useful when making maps. Pollution overview can also be used to see how much pollution buildings are producing."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Workspace]"
msgstr "Workspace"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Workspace]"
msgstr "**Workspace is the whole world area you have on your screen.** \r\n\r\nThe map controls and all the assets you have added or imported can be found here."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ImportingAssets]"
msgstr "Importing Assets"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ImportingAssets]"
msgstr "You can import your own 3D models and textures to be configured for in game use. "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ProjectRoot]"
msgstr "Project Root"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ProjectRoot]"
msgstr "Select the main folder where all your projects files and sub folders are located in."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[SelectedAssets]"
msgstr "Selected Assets"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[SelectedAssets]"
msgstr "Select the subfolder where the asset files you want to import are located in."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[SelectTemplate]"
msgstr "Select Template"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[SelectTemplate]"
msgstr "Select a similar asset to what you want to do as a template. This will copy the properties and layout of that asset to yours.\r\n\r\nYou can also start from an empty template to define your asset from scratch."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Import]"
msgstr "Import"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Import]"
msgstr "Select to start importing files to the template.\r\n\r\n**This process may take a short while.**"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ModifyingTheTerrain]"
msgstr "Modifying the Terrain"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ModifyingTheTerrain]"
msgstr "With the terrain tools you can build and modify the ground to make your own playable map for the game.\r\n\r\n**The entire map is the green area in your workspace and the marked area in the middle is the default starting area.**"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[TerrainTools]"
msgstr "Terrain Tools"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[TerrainTools]"
msgstr "Here are the tools to paint the ground to different shapes and heights. \r\n\r\n**When you have selected a tool, options for it will be shown at the bottom.**"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Heightmaps]"
msgstr "Heightmaps"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Heightmaps]"
msgstr "There are two different Heightmaps. The main Heightmap is for the playable map area, which is shown as a green plane, and the World heightmap is for the area outside your map. Area that can only be seen as the background.\r\n\r\nHeightmaps are grayscale images that can be used to create the grounds shape and height of the terrain. White areas will be the highest points and black areas the lowest points.\r\n\r\nYou can make a heightmap in any software that can save the image in the appropriate format."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[HeightScale]"
msgstr "Height Scale"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[HeightScale]"
msgstr "**The Height Scale number defines how high the highest point can be on your map, in meters.**\r\n\r\nIf you have made a heightmap image the white parts will be at the number you set. This will also set how high the ground can be build in game. \r\n\r\nDefault Height is 4096.000 and max is 10000.000\r\n**Note:** Extreme heights can cause grounds textures to stretch beyond what looks nice."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ImportHeightmap]"
msgstr "Import Heightmap"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ImportHeightmap]"
msgstr "Before you can import an image to be used as a heightmap, **you need to place the image in the game folder named Heightmaps.**\r\n\r\n**The heightmap must be in 4096x4096 pixels, grayscale image with 16 bit channel depth, in .png format.**\r\n\r\nWhen you select Import Heightmap all the images in that folder are shown and are available to be used. Select the one you want and click load."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ExportHeightmap]"
msgstr "Export Heightmap"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ExportHeightmap]"
msgstr "**You can save your current terrain as a Heightmap image for editing or future importing.**\r\n\r\nGive it a name to create a new Heightmap or select an existing file to overwrite it.\r\n\r\nExported Heightmaps can be found in the Heightmaps folder for later use."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ImportingAndExportingWorldmap]"
msgstr "Importing and Exporting Worldmap"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ImportingAndExportingWorldmap]"
msgstr "The same rules that apply to the main Heightmap also apply to the Worldmap image. See the previous steps for more details.\r\n\r\n**The Worldmap will extend outside the map area and provide the view of the land beyond your map. It will not affect the map area.**\r\n\r\n**Note:** It's good practise to save your progress often and have a way to go back to a previous point. It's also a good idea to export different versions of your heightmaps. "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[SculptingTheWorldmap]"
msgstr "Sculpting the Worldmap"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[SculptingTheWorldmap]"
msgstr "**To be able to sculpt the Worldmap area you will need to first import a Worldmap image.**\r\n\r\nAfter you have loaded the image you can work on the area with the terrain tools.\r\n \r\nIf there are abrupt height differences at the edge of map, you can sculpt the edge area with the terrain tools. "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[AddingObjects]"
msgstr "Adding Objects"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[AddingObjects]"
msgstr "Objects are models that are used in game or that you have created.\r\n\r\nYou can place object listed below to your workspace for propping or to be included in a map. "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Search]"
msgstr "Search"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Search]"
msgstr "You can search object by name or keyword, and apply filters to narrow down the results. "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Filter]"
msgstr "Filter"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Filter]"
msgstr "To view Specific types of assets, select one or more from the list to see only the selected types of assets."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Categories]"
msgstr "Categories"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Categories]"
msgstr "All available objects can also be found listed in the menu divided into categories."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ThumbnailSlider]"
msgstr "Thumbnail Slider"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ThumbnailSlider]"
msgstr "Slider for object thumbnail size.\r\n\r\nSlide right for a larger thumbnail and left for a list view."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Favorite]"
msgstr "Favorite"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Favorite]"
msgstr "**Select the star next an objects to find it quicker next time.**\r\n\r\nSelecting will favorite the asset and show a star next to it. All stared objects are found at the top of the list when you have All categories selected. "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Bulldozer]"
msgstr "Bulldozer"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Bulldozer]"
msgstr "Bulldozer is for deleting objects in your workspace."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ToolMode]"
msgstr "Tool Mode"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ToolMode]"
msgstr "Turn your prefered way of selecting objects On or Off."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[UndergroundMode]"
msgstr "Underground Mode"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[UndergroundMode]"
msgstr "Find things placed underground to delete."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Theme]"
msgstr "Theme"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Theme]"
msgstr "**Selecting a theme for your map will determine the default style of objects in game.**\r\n\r\nThe style of objects like roads and road decals, lights and trees along the roads and also the look of the traffic lights and bus stops. Default theme for zoning and signature buildings."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[StartingTiles]"
msgstr "Starting Tiles"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[StartingTiles]"
msgstr "**Starting Tiles are the area of the map that is open to be build on from the start of the game.**\r\n\r\nDefault is to have the middle 9 tiles open in a 3x3 square. You can choose any tiles and as many as you like. \r\n\r\nSelect with <inputAction:Tool/Apply> and deselect with <inputAction:Tool/Secondary Apply> "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[PreviewImage]"
msgstr "Preview Image"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[PreviewImage]"
msgstr "**Preview image is for showcasing your map.** To give a nice overview of what your map is like. \r\n\r\nPreview Image is a RGB color mode, 8 bits/channel, 687 x 384 pixels in .png format"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ThumbnailImage]"
msgstr "Thumbnail Image"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ThumbnailImage]"
msgstr "**Thumbnail Image is to identify your map** when selecting a new map at the start of a new game.\r\n\r\nRGB color mode, 8 bits/channel, 200x200 pixels in .png format"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Checklist]"
msgstr "Checklist "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Checklist]"
msgstr "For the map to function properly in the game, certain essential elements must be included.\r\n\r\nThen a few things that are nice to have.\r\n\r\n**The checklist will show you what you have placed in your map so you can make sure your map will work.**"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[WindSettings]"
msgstr "Wind Settings"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[WindSettings]"
msgstr "**Control the wind direction with the slider. Arrows are shown on the map for visual aid of the wind direction.**\r\n\r\nWind direction can be important when designing a map with great elevation differences. Wind direction can affect where good areas are for industrial zoning or where wind power stations are most efficient to place. "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ClimateSettings]"
msgstr "Climate Settings"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ClimateSettings]"
msgstr "**Climate settings include setting for what global area the map is situated in.**\r\n\r\nThese are setting that affect how cold or warm the map is and what position the sun is in, which affect how long the days and nights are."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Water]"
msgstr "Water"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Water]"
msgstr "**Water Settings are for all types of water that are present in the map.**\r\n\r\nWater for scenic purposes like lakes and ponds. Larger water ways like rivers and sea for boat and ship traffic. Most importantly water for citizens use.\r\n\r\n**Note:** For the map to work in game you must include at least one surface or groundwater source where the city can get its water supply."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[Resources]"
msgstr "Resources"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[Resources]"
msgstr "**Resources are materials found in the ground.**\r\n\r\nMaterials like water that the city needs for its citizens and companies to live and function.\r\n\r\nOther materials are used by industries, to cultivate the land or harvest the materials found there.  "

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ResourcesTools]"
msgstr "Resources Tools"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ResourcesTools]"
msgstr "**With the resource tools you can paint the area that will contain the selected material.**\r\n\r\nWhen a tool is selected Brush Tool Options are shown at the bottom.\r\n\r\nDifferent brush shapes are useful when painting mountains and other natural features with textured surfaces. Size, Strength and angel help with detailing the areas."

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.TITLE[ImportTexture]"
msgstr "Import Texture"

#: en
msgid "EditorTutorials.DESCRIPTION[ImportTexture]"
msgstr "Texture images for resources work the same as the heightmaps and need to be located in the same folder. The game folder named Heightmaps.\r\n\r\nSo before you import images, place the texture in the Heightmaps folder.\r\n\r\n**The images must be 4096x4096 pixels, grayscale image with 16 bit channel depth, in .png format.**\r\n\r\nWhen you select Import Heightmap all the images in that folder are shown and available to be used. Select the one you want and click load."

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadsTutorialPlacementGamepad]"
msgstr "Building Roads"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[RoadServicesTutorialRemoveSignGamepad]"
msgstr "Placing and Removing Signs and Decorations"

#: en
msgid "Tutorials.TITLE[ZoningTutorialRemoveZoneIntroGamepad]"
msgstr "Removing Zones"

#: en
